regress/gate_ab.sh:2:# GATE 7 -- three-way A/B against pristine 0.7.0 and the pre-batch fork.
regress/gate_ab.sh:3:#
regress/gate_ab.sh:4:#   pristine   39748e3   unmodified upstream GXemul 0.7.0
regress/gate_ab.sh:5:#   prebatch   2ffc91e   the fork immediately before the current batch
regress/gate_ab.sh:6:#   HEAD                 what is being shipped
regress/gate_ab.sh:7:#
regress/gate_ab.sh:8:# TWO DIFFERENT QUESTIONS. Conflating them is the trap this gate exists to avoid:
regress/gate_ab.sh:9:#
regress/gate_ab.sh:10:#   vs PRISTINE  = CAPABILITY PRESERVATION. The fork carries ~290 deliberate corrections,
regress/gate_ab.sh:11:#                  so differences are EXPECTED and are not regressions. The only thing
regress/gate_ab.sh:12:#                  that counts against us is a machine that ran under upstream and no
regress/gate_ab.sh:13:#                  longer runs.
regress/gate_ab.sh:14:#
regress/gate_ab.sh:15:#   vs PREBATCH  = CHANGE-SET VERIFICATION. Everything not touched by the batch should be
regress/gate_ab.sh:16:#                  identical, and anything that moved must be attributable to a specific
regress/gate_ab.sh:17:#                  correction.
regress/gate_ab.sh:18:#
regress/gate_ab.sh:19:# `gate_ab.sh --build` (re)creates the two baselines first; without it they are reused.
regress/gate_ab.sh:20:#
regress/gate_ab.sh:21:# METHOD NOTES, both learned the hard way:
regress/gate_ab.sh:22:#
regress/gate_ab.sh:23:#  * Every run goes through run_emu_progress() (#419; run_emu() no longer has any caller),
regress/gate_ab.sh:24:#    which forces stdbuf -o0. On a pipe, gxemul's stdout
regress/gate_ab.sh:25:#    is 4 KB block-buffered and `timeout`'s SIGTERM discards a partial block -- so a guest
regress/gate_ab.sh:26:#    that produced 3 KB of good output scores ZERO. An earlier version of this comparison
regress/gate_ab.sh:27:#    reported a "capability regression" on luna88k that was entirely a lost buffer.
regress/gate_ab.sh:28:#  * Comparison is on SEMANTIC MARKERS, never on byte counts. Under a wall-clock timeout a
regress/gate_ab.sh:29:#    byte count measures how fast the host happened to be, not what the guest did.
regress/gate_ab.sh:34:SEC=$ROOT/GXEMUL-SEC
regress/gate_ab.sh:68:# probe <label> <binary> <logfile> <args...>  -> prints "marker=N ..." for the rig
regress/gate_ab.sh:69:# NOTE THE R: PREFIX. It opens the base image read-only and routes every guest write into
regress/gate_ab.sh:70:# a temporary overlay that is discarded when the run ends.
regress/gate_ab.sh:71:#
regress/gate_ab.sh:72:# Without it this function booted THREE DIFFERENT BUILDS SEQUENTIALLY AGAINST ONE SHARED,
regress/gate_ab.sh:73:# WRITABLE 2 GB IMAGE, so the runs were not independent: each build inherited whatever
regress/gate_ab.sh:74:# filesystem state the previous one left behind, including an unclean unmount when the
regress/gate_ab.sh:75:# 300 s timeout killed a guest that had reached its login prompt. The symptom was a
regress/gate_ab.sh:76:# non-deterministic FAIL -- HEAD came back 1:1:0 having passed 1:1:1 twice at the same
regress/gate_ab.sh:77:# commit with no code change. The image's mtime confirmed it: it tracked the most recent
regress/gate_ab.sh:78:# boot, not the download.
regress/gate_ab.sh:79:#
regress/gate_ab.sh:80:# Timing was ruled out separately by measurement: both builds reach `login:` in about
regress/gate_ab.sh:81:# 100 s against a 300 s budget, so this was never a marginal timeout.
regress/gate_ab.sh:84:# #419: the 300 s wall budget above was replaced by a budget of GUEST WORK. See
regress/gate_ab.sh:85:# run_emu_progress() in lib.sh for the measurements. Three constants, each measured:
regress/gate_ab.sh:86:#
regress/gate_ab.sh:87:#   LUNA_BUDGET 12 G   Observed instructions-to-login spans 7.316-7.786 G over eleven boots.
regress/gate_ab.sh:88:#                      12 G is +54 % over the observed max. It bounds ONLY the failure path --
regress/gate_ab.sh:89:#                      a healthy boot stops at the MARKER long before it -- so over-provisioning
regress/gate_ab.sh:90:#                      costs nothing on the success path and removes per-host recalibration.
regress/gate_ab.sh:91:#                      8.5 G was rejected: it sits only ~5 % above the theoretical ceiling a
regress/gate_ab.sh:92:#                      maximally-precise usleep host would reach (~8.06 G).
regress/gate_ab.sh:93:#   LUNA_STALL  120 s  At the SLOWEST rate measured here (9.9 M instr/s -- 7,349,301,148
regress/gate_ab.sh:94:#                      instructions in 742 s, host saturated) one -N record arrives every
regress/gate_ab.sh:95:#                      3.4 s. That is a HANG, not a slow host -- but the claim is empirical,
regress/gate_ab.sh:96:#                      not absolute: this detector still reads `date +%s`, so a host that
regress/gate_ab.sh:97:#                      SUSPENDS or starves the process for two minutes looks identical.
regress/gate_ab.sh:98:#                      *** THE MULTIPLIER WAS OVERSTATED AS 35x. 3.4 s is a MEAN derived from
regress/gate_ab.sh:99:#                      total instructions over total wall time; the WORST observed gap is what
regress/gate_ab.sh:100:#                      matters, and measured directly on the pty path under saturation it is
regress/gate_ab.sh:101:#                      7.06 s (n=219: min 0.47, median 3.86, p90 5.54, p99 6.23). So the
regress/gate_ab.sh:102:#                      honest multiplier is 17x, not 35x. Still ample -- but a mean is not a
regress/gate_ab.sh:103:#                      worst case, and quoting one as the other is how a margin gets believed
regress/gate_ab.sh:104:#                      to be twice its real size. ***
regress/gate_ab.sh:105:#   LUNA_BACKSTOP 1800 s  2.4x the worst boot ever measured here (742 s, 8 busy loops on 8
regress/gate_ab.sh:106:#                      cores, which still reached login 1:1:1).
regress/gate_ab.sh:111:# *** THE CONSTANTS NEEDED THEIR OWN ROWS.  Measured in pass 2: multiplying LUNA_BUDGET by
