regress/gate_hygiene.sh:1:#!/bin/bash
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:2:# GATE 6 -- no distress markers in the boot logs.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:3:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:4:# Cheap, and it has caught real regressions: round 40 found that #265's own diagnostic
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:5:# flooded the log on a healthy boot, and that #261 was listing a subsystem it had not
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:6:# actually armed. Neither changed a pass/fail count -- both showed up only as marker counts.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:7:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:8:# READS THE RAW PTY LOGS ON PURPOSE. fatal() and debugmsg() output is not guest console
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:9:# output; it goes straight to stdout and interleaves into the pty stream, and the arc
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:10:# screen reconstruction that gate 4 needs for GUEST tokens actively garbles it. Measured:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:11:# '{ asc: data in' reads 0 from the arc screen rebuild and 5 from the raw log.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:12:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:13:# TWO WAYS THIS GATE USED TO PASS WITHOUT PROVING ANYTHING, both now closed:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:14:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:15:#  * EMPTY LOGS. It checked only that the files existed. `: > /tmp/pD.log` made 20 checks
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:16:#    go green -- two empty files certifying a clean boot. There is now a POSITIVE CONTROL:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:17:#    each log must be substantial AND contain the guest's own uid=0(root), so absence of
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:18:#    distress is only counted when presence of a real boot is proved.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:19:#  * STALE LOGS. Everything lives in /tmp and survives until reboot, so running this gate
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:20:#    alone -- or after gate 4 skipped -- graded yesterday's logs, produced by a binary that
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:21:#    no longer exists. Gate 4 now deletes them first and copies them into $LOGDIR when it
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:22:#    finishes; this gate reads THOSE copies, which only exist if gate 4 actually ran.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:29:# ---- static wiring: the readiness-predicate ratchet ---------------------
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:30:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:31:#  DELIBERATELY BEFORE the log check below, so a missing log can never SKIP
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:32:#  it.  This gate had no source assertions at all until now -- it graded logs
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:33:#  and nothing else -- which is why the defect it guards could spread unseen.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:34:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:35:#  THE DEFECT IT FREEZES: a readiness predicate matching a BARE ">" is
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:36:#  satisfied by the guest's own register dump, whose FIRST line is the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:37:#  symbol line "  <...>" -- and that line ends in ">".  Line-buffered on a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:38:#  pty, each line is its own flush, so a reader waking between line 1 and
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:39:#  line 2 returns SATISFIED WITH THE REGISTERS UNREAD.  Healthy host,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:40:#  microseconds wide, and armed on every CPU family whose dump starts that
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:41:#  way (MIPS, PPC, SH, m88k all do).
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:42:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:43:#  THE CONVERSION HAS LANDED (#392), so EXPECT_BARE is now 0 and this is a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:44:#  RATCHET AGAINST REGRESSION rather than against spread.  All 15 sites now take
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:45:#  a fresh mark before the write, require the FULL prompt in that slice, and
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:46:#  require the command's own echo first.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:47:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:48:#  The converted predicate deliberately keeps the LITERAL endswith("GXemul>")
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:49:#  spelling, which is the idiom the ARM probes already use (arm_idle_probe.py:194).
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:50:#  A reviewer proposed a shared helper holding the prompt in a constant or a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:51:#  regex; either would have broken this gate, though -- as a pass-2 seat pointed
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:52:#  out -- NOT by the same route, and the first draft of this comment conflated
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:53:#  them:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:54:#      endswith(PROMPT)  -> counted, unrecognised -> fails the `unknown` check;
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:55:#      re.search(...)    -> not an endswith at all, so `unknown` never moves --
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:56:#                           it fails the anchored-site COUNT below instead.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:57:#  Matching the house idiom kept the ratchet working with no redesign, which is
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:58:#  worth remembering the next time a "cleaner" abstraction is proposed for
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:59:#  something a static check is watching.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:60:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:61:#  Exact equality, not a ceiling, and on purpose: a "<=" check would let the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:62:#  number fall silently, and a silent fall is how you lose track of whether
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:63:#  the conversion actually happened.  Any movement in either direction must
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:64:#  be a deliberate edit here.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:65:#  COUNTING IT PROPERLY, after a review pass named three ways to walk around a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:66:#  naive grep: the single-quoted spelling, a variable or helper holding the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:67:#  prompt, and probes living in a subdirectory the flat glob never visits.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:68:#  A ratchet you can step around is worse than no ratchet, because it reads as
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:69:#  protection.  So:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:70:#    - find RECURSIVELY, not "$HERE"/*.py;
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:71:#    - accept either quote style;
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:72:#    - and make the check CLOSED-WORLD: every .endswith() in the tree must use
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:73:#      one of the two KNOWN literals.  A third spelling -- including
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:74:#      endswith(PROMPT) or any indirection through a helper -- is unknown, and
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:75:#      unknown fails.  That converts "I grepped for the bad thing" into "I
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:76:#      enumerated everything and recognised all of it", which is the only form
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:77:#      that cannot be evaded by inventing a new way to write it.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:80:#  CODE ONLY.  The first version of this check counted a line of PROSE -- a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:81:#  comment in arm_flags_probe.py that discusses endswith() -- and reported an
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:82:#  unrecognised spelling that does not exist.  A check that fires on its own
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:83:#  documentation trains the reader to ignore it, so full-line comments are
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:84:#  stripped before anything is counted.  (Known limit, stated rather than
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:85:#  hidden: an endswith() sitting after code on the same line as a trailing
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:86:#  comment is still counted as code, which is the safe direction.)
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:87:#  readiness_predicate_test.py is EXCLUDED here and pinned separately below.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:88:#  It is the offline truth table for #392, so it necessarily CONTAINS the two
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:89:#  broken spellings -- demonstrating them is its whole job. Counting them with
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:90:#  the live sites would make the ratchet permanently red.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:91:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:92:#  This is a FAIL-CLOSED exception, not a hole: the file is named exactly, and
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:93:#  its own contents are asserted below, so it cannot quietly stop testing what it
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:94:#  claims to test. A loose `grep -v test` would have been the wrong shape -- it
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:95:#  would silently exempt any future file with "test" in its name.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:96:#  *** SCOPE, MEASURED 2026-08-29: `regress/artifacts/` IS PRESERVED EVIDENCE, NOT LIVE
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:97:#  HARNESS CODE, and leaving it in scope was a defect in BOTH directions. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:98:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:99:#  *** THE NUMBERS THAT WERE HERE WENT STALE INSIDE THIS VERY DIFF, so they are
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:100:#  replaced by their DERIVERS.  An earlier draft froze 138 / 59 / 59; this round then
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:101:#  added wdc_latch_census.py and the true values became 139 / 60 / 60 -- a frozen count
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:102:#  in a comment ABOUT counting, wrong before the commit that wrote it had landed.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:103:#  Caught by the codex seat. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:104:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:105:#      find . -name "*.py" | wc -l                             the OLD recursive scope
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:106:#      find . -name "*.py" -not -path "*/artifacts/*" | wc -l  the NEW one
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:107:#      ls *.py | wc -l                                         the flat glob
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:108:#      grep -l "pty.fork" artifacts/*/*.py artifacts/*/*/*.py  the invisible copies
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:109:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:110:#  THE LOAD-BEARING FACT IS THE EQUALITY, NOT THE VALUES: recursive-minus-artifacts
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:111:#  equals the flat glob, which is what makes this the right exclusion -- it ALIGNS the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:112:#  censuses instead of moving any of them.  Re-derive rather than quote; if the two
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:113:#  ever differ, that difference is the finding.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:114:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:115:#  Two censuses recursed while a third used a flat glob -- which is evasion route #3
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:116:#  that this file condemns ninety lines above.  TEN pty-forking promptless probes sat
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:117:#  under artifacts/, invisible to the denominator, to the promptless pin and to the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:118:#  comm -13 offender-naming: invisible to the entire mechanism built to find them.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:119:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:120:#  AND THE HAZARD WAS SCHEDULED IN THE OTHER DIRECTION.  CLAUDE.md MANDATES frozen
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:121:#  probe copies for a comparison run; copying one converted probe into artifacts/
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:122:#  takes conv_anchor 23 -> 24 and reds this gate -- so the next round to freeze a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:123:#  probe would have gone red FOR OBEYING THE CARRIER.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:124:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:125:#  RECURSION IS KEPT so a genuinely new subdirectory is still scanned; only the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:126:#  evidence archive is excluded.  Measured a NO-OP on every pinned count today.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:130:#  -o, so these count OCCURRENCES and not LINES.  With -c a single line holding
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:131:#  two different spellings counts once in each of the three totals and drives
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:132:#  `unknown` NEGATIVE -- a subtraction is only meaningful if all three terms
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:133:#  count the same kind of thing.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:135:#  Every endswith( in code, minus the two we recognise: the bare prompt above
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:136:#  and the correct full prompt.  Anything left is a spelling nobody reviewed --
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:137:#  including endswith(PROMPT) or any indirection through a helper.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:139:full=$(py_code | grep -o "endswith([\"']GXemul>[\"'])" | wc -l)
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:144:#  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:145:#  *** THE THREE COUNTS ABOVE ARE CLOSED-WORLD AND STRUCTURALLY CANNOT SEE A
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:146:#  PROBE WITH NO PROMPT DETECTION AT ALL. ***  py_code() concatenates every
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:147:#  `endswith(` line across the tree, so a file containing ZERO of them
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:148:#  contributes to no counter and passes invisibly.  A ratchet built to catch
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:149:#  probes with BAD prompt detection is blind to probes with NONE -- which is
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:150:#  the larger set: SEVEN of the 33 pty-forking probes have no prompt
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:151:#  detection whatever.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:152:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:153:#  The fix is the one this project applies to every phantom-zero: ENUMERATE THE
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:154:#  DENOMINATOR FIRST.  Count the pty-forking probes, then the promptless subset,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:155:#  and compare that subset against an explicit list.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:156:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:157:#  *** IT IS A RATCHET, NOT A REJECT, AND THAT IS NOT A COMPROMISE. ***  EVERY
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:158:#  current probe is promptless, so "reject any file lacking a predicate" would
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:159:#  redden the whole battery PERMANENTLY -- a red no edit can clear, which this
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:160:#  project has already paid for once and which destroys the credibility of every
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:161:#  other row in the run.  The grandfathered set is named exactly, so it cannot
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:162:#  quietly grow; a NEW promptless probe fails, and converting one is a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:163:#  ratchet-down that must edit this list.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:164:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:165:#  Provenance: a codex seat named the enumerate-first shape on the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:166:#  probetimebased panel; the ratchet-not-reject refinement is the adjudication's,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:167:#  after it checked what a reject-all would do to the current set.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:168:#  *** BOTH PINS BELOW WERE RED AT HEAD UNTIL 2026-08-27, FOR THE SAME CAUSE, AND NOBODY
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:169:#  HAD SEEN IT. ***  Derived with this gate's own commands, not estimated:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:170:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:171:#      EXPECT_PTY_PROBES   pinned 33   actual 35
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:172:#      grandfathered set   pinned  7   actual  9
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:173:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:174:#  The two extra files are `asic_badaddr_probe.py` (#463) and `wdc_flood_probe.py` (#464).
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:175:#  Both were added AFTER the pins were last set and neither pin was bumped.  The weekly
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:176:#  battery runs Sundays, so the red had not surfaced -- and when it did it would have been
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:177:#  mis-attributed to whatever change triggered that run.  That is the same class #486 closed
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:178:#  for `overrun x2` on the same day: a scored row red for an unrelated, already-diagnosed
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:179:#  reason.  Found by a panel seat re-deriving the pin rather than reading it.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:180:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:181:#  *** ADDING THEM TO THE GRANDFATHERED SET IS NOT A BYPASS, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:182:#  WHOLE POINT OF THE ROW. ***  This list is not `probes we forgive`; it is `promptless
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:183:#  probes we KNOW about`, and the check's job is to NAME any that appear unnoticed (the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:184:#  `comm -13` below).  Both new entries are promptless for exactly the reason the five
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:185:#  already listed are -- they free-run to a breakpoint, and only a breakpoint hit returns a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:186:#  prompt, as `sh_intprio_probe.py` records in its own comment.  So they belong in the set,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:187:#  with the reason written down rather than the count quietly raised.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:188:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:189:#  *** THAT STATED REASON WAS WRONG ABOUT ITS OWN SET, AND IT SENT A READER TO THE WRONG
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:190:#  ONE OF THE TWO DISPOSITIONS THIS ROW OFFERS.  Censused member by member 2026-08-29,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:191:#  independently by three seats (codex, opus5, and the main loop), all agreeing: ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:192:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:193:#  *** THESE CATEGORIES OVERLAP.  THEY ARE NOT A PARTITION, AND AN EARLIER DRAFT OF
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:194:#  THIS BLOCK PRINTED THEM AS ONE -- 3 + 8 + 1 = 12 over a NINE-member set.  Caught on
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:195:#  pass 2 by the glm and agy seats, on the arithmetic. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:196:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:197:#      wall-clock BUDGET-DRAIN readiness .... 11 of 11   a `while ... time.time() ...` loop
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:198:#        of which ALSO free-run to a breakpoint  3       sh_intprio, sh_iprbcd, wdc_flood
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:199:#      NEITHER ............................... 0
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:200:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:201:#  *** THIS CENSUS HAS NOW BEEN WRONG THREE TIMES AND EACH ERROR WAS A DIFFERENT
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:202:#  SHAPE.  Recorded because the third one is the instructive one. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:203:#    (1) the ORIGINAL claimed a single universal reason ("they free-run to a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:204:#        breakpoint") that was true of 3 members.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:205:#    (2) MY FIRST CORRECTION printed 3 + 8 + 1 = 12 against a NINE-member set --
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:206:#        it read as a PARTITION when the categories overlap.  Caught on the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:207:#        arithmetic by the glm and agy seats.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:208:#    (3) MY SECOND CORRECTION said drive_guest.py is NEITHER.  It is not: it
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:209:#        budget-drains at `expect()` and its sibling, spelled
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:210:#        `while time.time() - t < timeout:`.  I had grepped for the literal
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:211:#        `time.time() < end`, which is ONE SPELLING of the mechanism, and
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:212:#        measured the spelling instead of the thing.  Caught by opus5.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:213:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:214:#  *** THAT IS THIS ROUND'S OWN DEFECT, COMMITTED IN THE COMMENT DESCRIBING IT:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:215:#  counting a TEXTUAL FORM where a PROPERTY was meant. ***  It is why the row-5
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:216:#  replacement counts set/read BALANCE rather than occurrences of a spelling.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:217:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:218:#  drive_guest.py IS in the promptless list, and for a reason that is about the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:219:#  CENSUS rather than about readiness: its `boot_progress()` matches with
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:220:#  `re.search`, which `grep -L "endswith("` cannot see.  That is why it is
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:221:#  LISTED -- not how it waits.  The two axes were conflated by (3).
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:222:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:223:#  Measured per member over the CURRENT eleven: breakpoint is a strict SUBSET of
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:224:#  budget-drain, so the set is covered by budget-drain alone and "neither" is empty.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:225:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:226:#  So the mechanism ALL ELEVEN share is BUDGET-DRAIN --
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:227:#  readiness by elapsed time rather than by prompt -- and the two probes added below share
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:228:#  it BYTE-FOR-BYTE with the already-listed gdrom_default_probe.py.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:229:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:230:#  *** drive_guest.py IS BUDGET-BEARING TOO; what is distinct about it is why it is
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:231:#  LISTED, not how it waits. ***  It is
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:232:#  classified promptless only because `grep -L "endswith("` cannot see REGEX readiness:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:233:#  its boot_progress() uses `re.search(pat, buf)`.  This gate's own comment already
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:234:#  anticipates that -- "re.search(...) -> not an endswith at all, so `unknown` never
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:235:#  moves" -- so the honest correction states the CENSUS rather than swapping one
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:236:#  universal claim for another.  A per-member reason, not a dominant one.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:237:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:238:#  The grandfather-with-a-reason discipline is UNCHANGED; only the reason is repaired.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:239:#  *** BOTH PINS RAISED 37->41 AND 11->15 ON 2026-08-31, AFTER RE-DERIVING THEM WITH THIS
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:240:#  GATE'S OWN COMPUTATION RATHER THAN APPROXIMATING IT.  Four pty-forking probes had been
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:241:#  added since the pins were set, all four promptless, so both pins were short by exactly
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:242:#  the same four -- which is the arithmetic that makes this one event and not two. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:243:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:244:#  Left alone, the next weekly battery reds TWO rows in this gate, and a failed gate is a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:245:#  failed battery.  This project has already had a battery go RED AND UNREAD FOR FIVE DAYS.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:246:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:247:#  *** PER-MEMBER REASONS, NOT A DOMINANT ONE -- the discipline this block already demands
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:248:#  of itself, and the four do NOT share a reason.  They fall into two groups: ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:249:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:250:#  BREAKPOINT-DRIVEN, i.e. promptless for exactly the reason the existing members are --
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:251:#  they free-run and only a breakpoint hit returns a prompt:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:252:#    wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py   its own comment states it: "The probe therefore breakpoints
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:253:#                              and continues."
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:254:#    wdc_altoffset_probe.py    sends `breakpoint add 0x%x` at HALT_ADDR and continues.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:255:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:256:#  TIME-BASED DRAIN, which is a WEAKER reason and is recorded as such rather than folded
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:257:#  in with the others -- these wait `while time.time() < end` around select() with no
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:258:#  prompt predicate at all:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:259:#    gdrom_cond_probe.py
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:260:#    sgi_re_width_probe.py     *** ADDED BY #497 ON 2026-08-30, i.e. BY THE SESSION THAT
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:261:#                              RAISED THIS PIN.  Disclosed rather than absorbed. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:262:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:263:#  The second group is the live defect tracked by the `probetimebased` queue row.  Listing
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:264:#  them here does not forgive them: this set is `promptless probes we KNOW about`, and the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:265:#  `comm -13` below exists to NAME any that appear unnoticed.  A probe in this list with a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:266:#  weak reason is tracked; the same probe absent from it is invisible.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:267:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:268:#  PROVENANCE, DERIVED FROM git RATHER THAN ASSUMED -- because "both pins are short by the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:269:#  same four" is an assumption until the sets are compared.  Against d9efd33, the commit that
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:270:#  last set EXPECT_PTY_PROBES=37 (VERIFIED: `git show d9efd33:regress/gate_hygiene.sh'):
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:271:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:272:#  *** THIS NUMBER READ 38 UNTIL #500 pass 11, AND THE #500 CHAIN IS WHAT BROKE IT.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:273:#  It was 37, which was CORRECT, and a comment edit in this chain changed a right
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:274:#  number into a wrong one.  That is the carrier's "a correction is not safer than
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:275:#  the thing it corrects" class, in its worst direction: not a stale number left
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:276:#  standing, but a CORRECT one overwritten. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:277:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:278:#    non-artifact .py under regress/ :  60 -> 66   (SIX new files, not four)
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:279:#    of the six, pty.fork            :  FOUR       gdrom_cond, sgi_re_width,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:280:#                                                  wdc_addtoinbuf, wdc_altoffset
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:281:#    of those four, endswith(        :  ZERO       so all four are promptless
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:282:#    not in the denominator          :  wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe.py,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:283:#                                       wdc_altoffset_sig_probe.py -- they fork no pty
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:284:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:285:#  So both pins move by the SAME four files, +4 each, and the two extra new probes are
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:286:#  correctly outside the denominator.  Had one of the six been a pty probe WITH readiness,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:287:#  the two deltas would have differed and this raise would have been wrong.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:288:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:289:#  NEGATIVE CONTROLS RUN BEFORE COMMITTING (recorded, not asserted): with the four added
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:290:#  both rows go green; removing any ONE of the four reds the promptless row again with
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:291:#  got=15 want=14, so the check still fires rather than having been switched off.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:292:#  *** RAISED 41 -> 42 ON 2026-08-31, AND IT IS THE SECOND TIME IN ONE SESSION THAT A ROUND
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:293:#  OF MINE ADDED A PROBE AND LEFT THIS PIN SHORT. ***  The 42nd is
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:294:#  `sgi_de_width_probe.py', shipped by #498 hours after the same session raised this pin
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:295:#  37 -> 41 for exactly this reason.  The weekly battery of 2026-08-30 18:00 had ALREADY
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:296:#  red on the earlier instance (got=40 want=37, got=14 want=11) and that verdict sat unread
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:297:#  for ten hours; this one was caught by running the dead-man switch rather than by waiting
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:298:#  for Sunday.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:299:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:300:#  *** THE LESSON IS NOT `BE MORE CAREFUL'.  A ROUND THAT ADDS A pty-FORKING PROBE MUST
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:301:#  RAISE THIS PIN IN THE SAME COMMIT ***, because the gate cannot fire until the weekly
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:302:#  battery runs -- so the feedback arrives up to seven days after the mistake, by which time
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:303:#  the round that made it is closed.  Re-derive, never transcribe:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:304:#    find regress -name '*.py' ! -path '*/artifacts/*' | xargs grep -l pty.fork | wc -l
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:305:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:306:#  *** BOTH HALVES WERE STALE, NOT ONE.  An earlier draft of this very comment asserted that
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:307:#  the promptless half `needed no change' because #498 had added the probe to the list below.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:308:#  IT HAD NOT.  Checked before committing: the list held 15 members and the measured
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:309:#  promptless set held 16, the extra being sgi_de_width_probe.py -- so that check would have
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:310:#  red too, at got=16 want=15. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:311:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:312:#  The retraction is kept because the near-miss is the point: I wrote a confident claim about
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:313:#  a list, in a comment, WITHOUT OPENING THE LIST -- one line after correcting a stale pin
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:314:#  caused by the same habit.  The check that caught it cost one `grep -c`.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:315:#  *** RAISED 42 -> 43 BY #499, IN THE SAME COMMIT THAT ADDS THE PROBE. ***  The 43rd is
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:316:#  `sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py'.  This is the THIRD raise in one session and the FIRST that
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:317:#  did not arrive as a surprise: the two before it were found by the weekly battery and by
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:318:#  a re-derivation hours after the fact, because this check cannot fire until the battery
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:319:#  runs, so the feedback lands up to seven days late -- after the round that caused it has
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:320:#  closed.  The obligation was written into
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:321:#  regress/artifacts/sgireunmapped/killtable_20260831/port/WIRING_OBLIGATIONS.md at wiring
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:322:#  time and predicted BOTH pins correctly.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:323:#  *** RAISED 43 -> 44 BY #500 pass 11, AND THE RAISE IS THE FINDING: #500 ADDED A
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:324:#  pty.fork PROBE, EDITED THIS COMMENT TO NAME IT, AND NEVER TOUCHED THE PIN.  So
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:325:#  `readiness: pty-probe denominator' HAS BEEN FAILING SINCE 840f3d0. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:326:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:327:#  The 44th is `sgi_de_primop_probe.py'.  Measured before the edit, with the gate's
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:328:#  OWN enumerator rather than a flat glob:  _ptyn = 44, pin = 43, check FAILS.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:329:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:330:#  *** WHY NOBODY SAW IT, and this is the part worth carrying: THIS CHECK ONLY RUNS
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:331:#  IN THE FULL WEEKLY BATTERY, AND THE WEEKLY BATTERY IS DISABLED BY OWNER CHOICE.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:332:#  A disabled battery produces NO RED ROW ANYWHERE -- nothing can observe its own
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:333:#  absence.  The checkpoint has carried that hazard as a standing warning for days;
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:334:#  this is its first MEASURED instance, and it cost a red gate that shipped in nine
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:335:#  consecutive commits without a single signal. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:336:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:337:#  DERIVE IT, never transcribe it -- a count in a durable record names its deriver:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:338:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:339:#      find regress -name '*.py' -type f ! -path 'regress/artifacts/*' \
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:340:#          | xargs grep -l 'pty.fork' | wc -l
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:341:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:342:#  (The `! -path artifacts' exclusion is load-bearing: the artifacts tree holds
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:343:#  FROZEN probe copies, which are evidence rather than gate material.)
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:344:#  *** RAISED 44 -> 46 BY pcc2widthkills, IN THE SAME COMMIT THAT ADDS THE TWO FILES --
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:345:#  pcc2_width_witness.py and pcc2_width_probe.py, both pty.fork, both promptless (fixed
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:346:#  timeouts, no endswith( predicate).  Written into the checkpoint at the moment the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:347:#  witness was created, because #500 added a probe and moved NEITHER census, and it took
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:348:#  two passes and three seats to find. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:350:#  *** AND THE SAME PROBE BROKE A SECOND CENSUS IN THIS FILE, WHICH pass 11's OWN
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:351:#  REPAIR DID NOT FIX.  Found by the codex seat reviewing that repair. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:352:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:353:#  `#500' added `sgi_de_primop_probe.py', and it broke BOTH pty censuses here:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:354:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:355:#      readiness: pty-probe denominator            44 vs 43   <- pass 11 fixed this
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:356:#      readiness: promptless probes, grandfathered 18 vs 17   <- AND MISSED THIS
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:357:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:358:#  *** THAT IS THIS ROUND'S OWN HEADLINE FAILURE, COMMITTED INSIDE THE REPAIR FOR
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:359:#  IT.  Pass 11 diagnosed "I close one side of a symmetry and leave the mirror
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:360:#  open", raised the pin, and left the sibling census in the SAME FILE untouched --
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:361:#  a second red that shipped in the same nine commits and survived the pass that
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:362:#  was sweeping for exactly this. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:363:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:364:#  Grandfathered DELIBERATELY, which is one of the two dispositions this gate's own
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:365:#  message offers ("add prompt detection, or grandfather deliberately").  The
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:366:#  statement is TRUE rather than convenient: the probe's `session()' drives the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:367:#  guest on fixed `settle'/`spin' timeouts and has no prompt predicate at all --
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:368:#  `grep -c "endswith(" sgi_de_primop_probe.py' is 0.  So it belongs on this list
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:369:#  exactly as the other seventeen do.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:370:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:371:#  ADDING REAL READINESS IS FILED, NOT DONE, and the reason is scope rather than
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:372:#  difficulty: changing how that probe decides a command finished perturbs all 47
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:373:#  rows and owes a full re-measured kill table.  That is a round, not a records fix,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:374:#  and doing it inside a records pass is how a green detector becomes unverified.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:382:#  *** THIS WAS THE THIRD POPULATION AND THE F2 EDIT MISSED IT.  Found by the agy seat
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:383:#  on pass 2. ***  py_code and probe_code were converted to recursive-minus-artifacts
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:384:#  while this line kept a FLAT GLOB -- so two censuses were aligned and a third was not,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:385:#  which is the exact defect F2 exists to end.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:386:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:387:#  It AGREES today (recursive-minus-artifacts == flat == 59) and that agreement is a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:388:#  COINCIDENCE, not a property: a .py added in any NEW non-artifacts subdirectory would
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:389:#  be scanned by two censuses and missed by this one.  Equal counts are not an aligned
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:390:#  population, and reading the equality as safety is how the flat glob survived the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:391:#  first repair.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:404:#  Name the offender rather than only the count -- a bare number tells nobody
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:405:#  WHICH file, and the whole point of the denominator is attribution.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:415:#  THE POSITIVE HALF OF THE #392 CHECK, and it is the half that binds the offline
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:416:#  truth table in gate_offline.sh to the code that actually ships.  Counting only
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:417:#  the ABSENCE of the bad spelling is not enough: a site could be deleted, or
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:418:#  reverted to something that is neither the old form nor the new one, and the two
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:419:#  checks above would both stay green.  So count the three constructs the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:420:#  conversion introduced and require all fifteen of each.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:421:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:422:#  Exact equality again, and for the same reason as EXPECT_BARE: a ">=" would let
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:423:#  a site quietly disappear.  If a probe legitimately gains or loses a wait site,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:424:#  this number is edited deliberately, in the same commit, by someone who looked.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:425:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:426:#  14 -> 15 on 2026-08-16: m8820x_sites_probe.py.  It shipped in 9494c6a with the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:427:#  anchored form and the echo guard but NOT the pinned call form -- so two of the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:428:#  three counts went to 15 and this gate went red, correctly, on a commit that had
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:429:#  never been run past it.  The probe is converted rather than exempted, and the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:430:#  conversion was not free: reordering wait()'s parameters turned an existing
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:431:#  positional wait(120) into mark=120, which is a real behaviour change and is
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:432:#  fixed in the same commit.  That is the argument for the exact-equality pin.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:433:#  15 -> 16 on 2026-08-19: footbridge_sites_probe.py, the first reachability witness this tree
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:434:#  has ever had for a footbridge machine.  MEASURED with this gate's own probe_code() census on
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:435:#  a staged copy: conv_anchor, conv_echo and conv_mark all move 15 -> 16 while bare, unknown and
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:436:#  whole_full do not move at all.  The probe ships WITH the pinned #392 call form rather than
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:437:#  being converted afterwards -- which is the lesson of the m8820x entry above, where a probe
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:438:#  landed in 9494c6a carrying two of the three constructs and turned this gate red on a commit
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:439:#  that had never been run past it.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:440:#  16 -> 17 on 2026-08-19: luna_intmask_probe.py, #438's witness.  *** AND THIS ENTRY IS THE
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:441:#  THIRD TIME THIS GATE HAS GONE RED FOR THE SAME REASON, ONE COMMIT AFTER THE SECOND. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:442:#  cf4b083 added the probe carrying all three pinned constructs and did NOT move this number.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:443:#  The commit ran gate 2 and the float selftest; GATE 6 NEVER FIRED, so the red was never seen
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:444:#  and shipped latent -- it would have failed the Aug 22 weekly battery as a hygiene regression
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:445:#  with a bookkeeping cause, which is the phantom-regression class this project already names.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:446:#  Found by the flagship seat recomputing this gate's own probe_code() census read-only.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:447:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:448:#  The m8820x entry above records this shape, the footbridge entry directly above records it
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:449:#  again, AND THE FOOTBRIDGE ENTRY WAS WRITTEN THE SAME DAY -- by the same operator, who was
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:450:#  warned about the coupling for THAT probe, handled it correctly, and then repeated it for a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:451:#  different probe one commit later.  Reading a lesson is not the same as having it.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:452:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:453:#  The structural fix, since three annotations have not been enough: a probe that ships in the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:454:#  same commit as its gate wiring cannot skip this pin, because the wiring forces a gate run.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:455:#  cf4b083 shipped the probe UNWIRED, so nothing made gate 6 fire.  See the luna88k block in
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:456:#  gate_arm.sh, added with this bump.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:457:#  17 -> 18 on 2026-08-20: pit8253_latch_probe.py, #439's witness for pitlatch/pitclobber.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:458:#  MEASURED read-only with this gate's OWN probe_code()/py_code() definitions, run before and
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:459:#  after staging the file: conv_anchor, conv_echo and conv_mark all move 17 -> 18 while bare,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:460:#  unknown and whole_full do not move at all (0 / 0 / 2 both times) -- the same signature the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:461:#  footbridge entry above records.  Bumped in the SAME edit that added the probe, which is the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:462:#  coupling the three entries above went red for.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:463:#  The probe ships UNWIRED.  Per the structural note directly above, that means nothing forces
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:464:#  gate 6 to fire, and an unwired probe is exactly what shipped latent in cf4b083 -- so this pin
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:465:#  is doing the whole job here and was verified by recomputation rather than by a gate run.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:466:#  Wiring it into gate_arm.sh alongside footbridge_sites_probe.py is the follow-up, and it wants
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:467:#  an operator who can run that gate.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:468:#  18 -> 19 on 2026-08-20: sh4_bsc_width_probe.py, #441's detector for sh4bcr.  MEASURED the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:469:#  same way -- this gate's own probe_code()/py_code(), run before and after staging: all three of
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:470:#  conv_anchor, conv_echo and conv_mark move 18 -> 19 while bare, unknown and whole_full do not
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:471:#  move (0/0/2).  The same signature every entry above records.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:472:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:473:#  IT DID NOT START THAT WAY, and the near-miss is the part worth keeping.  The probe first named
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:474:#  its local `mark` rather than `_mark`, so the fresh-mark guard was PRESENT but invisible to
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:475:#  conv_mark, which keys on `return wait(mark=_mark, ...)`.  Two counts went green and the third
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:476:#  went red -- and the first draft of THIS comment argued that was correct and blamed the census,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:477:#  on the reasoning that renaming a local to satisfy a grep is the tail wagging the dog.  Wrong:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:478:#  `_mark` is the idiom in every other pty probe in the tree, so the new file was the outlier.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:479:#  Teaching the census a second spelling would have made it count substrings of the first.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:480:#  19 -> 20 on 2026-08-20: fbpending_drain_probe.py, #442's rung-3 reproduction.  All three
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:481:#  counts move together after two corrections made in the same edit: the probe was renamed
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:482:#  `mark` -> `_mark` (the sh4markspell shape, one round old), and conv_mark's pattern was
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:483:#  extended to see the METHOD form, because this is the first class-based pty probe in the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:484:#  tree and its guard was invisible to a pattern written for closures.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:485:#  20 -> 21 on 2026-08-21: fbpending_witness.py, #442's rung-3 reproduction, COMMITTED as part
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:486:#  of that round's pass 2.  It had lived in _scratchpad/, so a clone could not re-run the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:487:#  reproduction that licensed the fix -- the mrwstore2 shape, whose probe left no artefact at all.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:488:#  All three counts move together (21/21/21): it uses the closure form and the `_mark` spelling,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:489:#  so neither of the two corrections the previous entry needed applies here.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:490:#  21 -> 22 on 2026-08-21: sh4_pcic_probe.py, #443's detector.  All three counts move together
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:491:#  (22/22/22) and bare/unknown/whole_full do not move (0/0/2) -- the signature every prior entry
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:492:#  records.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:493:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:494:#  *** THIS PIN WAS LATENTLY RED AT HEAD BETWEEN 3f9ff56 AND THIS COMMIT, AND THE CAUSE WAS A
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:495:#  CONCURRENCY SLIP RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT ABOUT THE COUPLING. ***  A `git add -A regress/`
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:496:#  during the #442 pass-2 commit swept in a probe a background agent was still creating, so the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:497:#  file landed WITHOUT its pin and WITHOUT this annotation.  That is the FOURTH recurrence of the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:498:#  coupling these comments already document (m8820x, footbridge, luna_intmask, sh4_bsc_width) --
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:499:#  and the first caused by staging rather than by forgetting.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:500:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:501:#  The structural note above says a probe shipping in the same commit as its gate wiring cannot
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:502:#  skip the pin, because the wiring forces a gate run.  `git add -A` defeats that by adding files
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:503:#  the author did not choose.  STAGE BY EXPLICIT PATH when a background agent may be writing into
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:504:#  the same directory.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:505:#  22 -> 23 in the SAME commit: sh4pcic_witness.py, #443's rung-3 reproduction, committed
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:506:#  alongside its detector.  It is a SEPARATE artefact from sh4_pcic_probe.py and both belong in
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:507:#  the tree: the witness asserts the PRE-FIX symptom (so it correctly reads 15/30 now) and the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:508:#  detector asserts the FIXED behaviour.  Grading one by the other's clauses is a category error.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:509:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:510:#  Committing it is the mrwstore2 lesson: that round's rung-3 probe left NO ARTEFACT, so its
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:511:#  witness became a remembered grep and a later row had to re-cost the work.  #442's witness had
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:512:#  the same problem and was committed one commit ago for the same reason.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:514:#  One helper for all three, so they agree on WHAT they look at.  The first draft
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:515:#  used py_code() for the anchored count and a raw grep for the other two, which
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:516:#  meant a comment mentioning the echo guard would have inflated one count and not
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:517:#  the others -- a spurious mismatch with no defect behind it.  A seat caught it
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:518:#  while it was still latent.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:519:#  Same scope as py_code above -- the two must agree, and a past defect came from
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:520:#  their populations diverging.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:524:conv_anchor=$(probe_code 'resp.rstrip().endswith("GXemul>")' | grep -c .)
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:526:#  ANCHOR THROUGH THE ECHO ARGUMENT.  The first version stopped at
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:527:#  'return wait(mark=_mark' -- a PREFIX -- so deleting ", echo=s if s else None"
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:528:#  from all fourteen sites left this count at 14 and every other counter
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:529:#  unchanged. A pass-2 seat MEASURED that mutant passing gate_hygiene,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:530:#  gate_offline, gate_mips_rounding and gate_sh_rounding simultaneously: a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:531:#  shipped fix with no detector, which is this project's worst vacuity class.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:532:#  The comma is what makes the assertion an assertion.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:533:#  ANCHOR THROUGH THE WHOLE EXPRESSION, not just as far as "echo=". A pass-2b
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:534:#  seat pointed out that the previous form still admitted
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:535:#      return wait(mark=_mark, echo=None)
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:536:#  which passes the argument and disables the guard in ONE WORD. This is the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:537:#  THIRD tightening of this same count -- first it stopped at "_mark", then at
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:538:#  "echo=", now at the full conditional. Each time the surviving prefix was a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:539:#  real substring of both the fixed and the broken code, which is exactly the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:540:#  property a detector must not have.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:541:#  *** EXTENDED 2026-08-20 FOR THE METHOD FORM, and narrowly on purpose given the history
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:542:#  in the paragraph above. ***  Every probe until now wrote `send()` as a closure over a local
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:543:#  `buf`, so the guard read `return wait(mark=_mark, ...)`.  fbpending_drain_probe.py is the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:544:#  first CLASS-BASED probe in the tree: its guard is `return self.wait(mark=_mark, ..., 
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:545:#  timeout=timeout)`, which is not a spelling variation but a different construct, and the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:546:#  pattern could not see it AT ALL -- the guard was present, correct, and uncounted.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:547:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:548:#  That is a wider blind spot than the `mark`/`_mark` one filed as sh4markspell: a whole probe
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:549:#  STYLE was invisible.  The two additions here are `\(self\.\)\?` and an optional trailing
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:550:#  argument.  Both are anchored -- `mark=_mark` and the full `echo=` conditional still have to
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:551:#  be present and in order -- so neither re-opens the hole the three earlier tightenings closed:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:552:#  a site that passes the argument, or drops the conditional, still fails to match.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:555:#  THE HOLE A PASS-2 SEAT FOUND, and it is the sharpest finding of the review:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:556:#  the checks above catch a REVERT but not an ADDITION of the OTHER broken form.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:557:#  A brand-new site spelled
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:558:#        if buf.rstrip().endswith("GXemul>"):
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:559:#  passes everything -- bare is still 0, the spelling is RECOGNISED so `unknown`
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:560:#  stays 0, and it is not the anchored form so conv_anchor stays 14.  Yet #392
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:561:#  MEASURED that exact configuration (full prompt, whole buffer) failing just as
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:562:#  completely as the bare one: arm B scored 0/80, byte-identical to arm A.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:563:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:564:#  So count it explicitly.  The two that exist are arm_flags_probe.py:144 and
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:565:#  :645 -- known, filed, and deliberately out of #392's scope.  Pinning the count
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:566:#  at 2 turns that scope decision into a FAIL-CLOSED ALLOWLIST: those two are
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:567:#  tolerated, a third is not, and when they are finally converted this number
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:568:#  goes to 0 in the same commit.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:570:#  *** ROW 3: SCOPED TO FILES THAT CAN DRIVE A REAL GUEST, rather than exempting a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:571:#  filename.  Adjudicated 2026-08-29; the name-exclusion form was WITHDRAWN. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:572:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:573:#  The allowlist below is FAIL-CLOSED over a pattern #392 MEASURED at 0/80, so the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:574:#  disposition for a new hit is never to raise the pin.  The two that appeared are
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:575:#  `selftest_inactivity.py:87` -- a deliberately preserved negative control whose own
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:576:#  docstring says DO NOT FIX IT -- and `:102`, #467's correct shape.  NEITHER DRIVES A
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:577:#  GUEST: both read a Stub that pops a python list, so #392's failure mode (a real
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:578:#  guest's own output racing the predicate) cannot arise.  That file is also LIVE
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:579:#  APPARATUS -- gate_offline.sh runs it every battery -- so it must not be edited.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:580:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:581:#  A filename exemption was the wrong shape and this file says so at :92-95 and
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:582:#  :347-348: an exemption must travel WITH a content pin on the excluded file.  Scoping
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:583:#  the POPULATION needs neither an exemption nor a companion pin, and it subsumes the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:584:#  readiness_predicate_test.py case automatically.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:585:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:586:#  THE POPULATION TERM IS TWO ALTERNATIVES, and the second is not hypothetical:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:587:#  `selftest_absorb.py`, `selftest_budget.py` and `selftest_logdir.py` import
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:588:#  drive_guest and contain NO pty.fork literal -- measured -- so a pty.fork-only term
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:589:#  would let a guest-driving file out of the census.  None holds a whole-full site
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:590:#  today; the term is widened so that it cannot.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:591:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:592:#  *** THE CLAIM IS SCOPED, NOT GENERAL, AND TWO SEATS SPLIT ON IT. ***  codex named
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:593:#  three files that drive the emulator with NEITHER spelling -- hpcabort_witness.py,
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:594:#  hpcmips_ctor_probe.py and sgi_eaddr_probe.py, through subprocess.run or GDB --args.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:595:#  So this is NOT a general guest-driving census and must not be read as one.  grok
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:596:#  argued against widening to cover them and that is the disposition taken: they
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:597:#  cannot carry the #392 failure, which is a PTY READINESS RACE, and all three hold
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:598:#  zero `endswith(` in any case (measured).  Widening to processes that cannot exhibit
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:599:#  the defect would make this pin answer a question it is not asking.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:600:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:601:#  DISCLOSED RESIDUALS, all real, none live today: `from pty import fork` with a bare
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:602:#  fork(); `import pty as p`; a statement-local import; `os.forkpty` directly (which is
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:603:#  what pty.fork wraps); `import regress.drive_guest`.  Censused at 0 apiece.  THE
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:604:#  PRESENT VALUE OF 2 IS CORRECT; THAT DOES NOT ESTABLISH POPULATION COMPLETENESS.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:605:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:606:#  `-print0 | xargs -0` matches both neighbours: a bare xargs finds ZERO files when a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:607:#  path contains a space (measured).  No space in $HERE today; the neighbours' own
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:608:#  comment says the three must agree, so it agrees.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:609:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:610:#  NO FILENAME EXEMPTION HERE.  readiness_predicate_test.py matches neither alternative
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:611:#  and holds zero whole-full sites, so scoping subsumes it -- an earlier draft carried
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:612:#  the exemption anyway, inert and un-companioned by a content pin, which is the shape
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:613:#  :92-95 condemns.  opus5 found it dead and it is gone.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:619:whole_full=$(pty_code 'buf.rstrip().endswith("GXemul>")' | grep -c .)
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:625:#  PIN THE EXEMPTED FILE'S OWN CONTENTS.  readiness_predicate_test.py is excluded
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:626:#  from the census above because it deliberately contains the broken spellings;
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:627:#  that exemption is only safe while the file still HOLDS them.  If its two
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:628:#  negative arms were deleted the offline truth table would go green by testing
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:629:#  nothing, and the exclusion above would hide it.  So the exemption and this
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:630:#  assertion travel together -- an allowlist entry that checks what it exempts.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:631:#  -o again, not -c: OCCURRENCES, not lines.  Same reason as above, and the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:632:#  expected numbers are asymmetric for a real reason -- 2 bad arms, but 3 good
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:633:#  occurrences, because besides the two `full-*` arms the test also prints the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:634:#  LEFTOVER demonstration line, which re-matches the prompt on purpose to show
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:635:#  that rstrip() erases the trailing space.  The first draft of this check
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:636:#  expected 2 and failed; the file was right and the expectation was wrong.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:637:#  CODE ONLY here too.  The first version grepped the file RAW, so a seat
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:638:#  gutted both negative arms, dropped the two spellings into a single COMMENT
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:639:#  line, and the pin still read 2 and 3. The filter that this file already
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:640:#  applies sixty lines above was missing from the one check whose whole job is
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:641:#  to keep an exemption honest.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:646:#  FIVE, not three: the two original full-* arms, the two late-prompt arms that
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:647:#  give the ECHO conjunct its behavioural coverage, and the leftover demo.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:649:      "$( [ -f "$rpt" ] && rpt_code | grep -o 'endswith("GXemul>")' | wc -l || echo missing)" "5"
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:651:#  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:652:#  A CLASS RATCHET, not a bug fix.  Task from the R6 pass-2 kimi seat, whose
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:653:#  answer had gone unread for a day: "sweep for other divisions in
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:654:#  debug()/fatal() argument position with guest-writable operands -- ns16550 is
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:655:#  unlikely to be the only instance of that shape."
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:656:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:657:#  The shape is worth a ratchet because C evaluates an argument whether or not
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:658:#  the callee prints it, so a division there runs at EVERY verbosity.  A grep
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:659:#  cannot find it: the operator has to be in argument position with literals and
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:660:#  comments already stripped, and the call may span lines.  diag_div_sweep.py
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:661:#  balances the parens and does that; it also splits constant divisors (which
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:662:#  cannot be zero, so are not the shape) from drivable ones.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:663:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:664:#  MEASURED 2026-08-16: 24 sites, 22 constant, 2 non-constant -- so kimi's
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:665:#  hypothesis is REFUTED, and the ratchet is what keeps the refutation true.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:666:#    * dev_ns16550.c, anchor `115200 / d->divisor` -- the live one, filed `ns16550`.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:667:#      The other 115200 in that file is `115200 / 9600`, a CONSTANT divisor and not
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:668:#      the shape, so anchor on the `d->divisor` form rather than on the number.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:669:#    * diskimage.c, anchor `(int) (bytes_left % ct->sector_size)` -- NOT drivable:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:670:#      every unsupported TRACK mode reaches a `goto fail` immediately following the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:671:#      diagnostic anchored at `unsupported TRACK mode`, and `nr_of_pt ++` runs only
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:672:#      after that, so a counted track always carries 2048/2336/2352.  The plain
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:673:#      division -- anchor `nr_sectors = bytes_left / ct->sector_size` -- would fault
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:674:#      first regardless.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:675:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:676:#  *** THESE WERE LINE NUMBERS UNTIL 2026-08-30 AND THREE OF THE FIVE HAD DRIFTED. ***
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:677:#  `dev_ns16550.c:79` had become a comment line (the division is at :181);
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:678:#  `diskimage.c:543` was wrong by 440 lines (:983); and `:819` named the LAST ARGUMENT
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:679:#  LINE of the diagnostic rather than the `goto fail` below it -- the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:680:#  read-the-line-before-citing-it trap, inside a citation whose only job is to be
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:681:#  re-checked.  `:823` and `:974` were still correct, so this is drift and not a bad
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:682:#  transcription.  Every anchor above was verified to match EXACTLY ONCE when written;
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:683:#  the bare form `bytes_left % ct->sector_size` matches TWICE and is deliberately not
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:684:#  used.  An anchor that stops matching is LOUD; a drifted number is silent.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:685:#  A THIRD site means someone added a new instance of the class: read it before
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:686:#  raising this number.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:740:    gate_skip "no logs from this run -- gate 4 must run first (it publishes them here)"
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:742:# ---- positive control --------------------------------------------------
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:743:# Without this the gate cannot tell "nothing went wrong" from "nothing happened".
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:744:# The floor is MEASURED, not guessed. A healthy run produces about 4,600 bytes on pmax
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:745:# (a plain serial console) and about 1,195,000 on arc (a VGA text console that repaints
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:746:# differentially, so it re-emits the screen constantly). An earlier draft used a single
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:747:# 20,000-byte floor for both and failed pmax on a perfectly good boot -- a false red, which
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:748:# is how gates get switched off. 2,000 is comfortably below the smaller rig and still far
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:749:# above the "empty or truncated file" case this control exists to catch.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:754:# pmax sets the 8th bit on every console character; arc repaints differentially. Strip the
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:755:# high bit for pmax and accept either the raw arc log or gate 4's screen rebuild.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:762:# ---- markers that must be ZERO -----------------------------------------
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:763:# Every pattern below was verified to exist as an emittable string in src/. Three did NOT
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:764:# survive that check and were removed rather than left in place looking like coverage:
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:765:#   'FIFO underrun'        -- 0 sites. The real string is 'FIFO overrun' (4 sites).
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:766:#   'INSTR: unimplemented' -- 0 sites. The token 'INSTR:' is in no format string at all;
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:767:#                             the real dyntrans marker is 'could not find physical'.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:768:#   '{ asc: data in'       -- exists, but is NOT distress. See the bounded list below.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:791:# ---- the OTHER rigs' logs ----------------------------------------------
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:792:# This gate used to read only the two MIPS pty logs, so a panic in the m88k or SuperH
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:793:# guest left it green while the README claimed "any boot log". Gate 5's logs are scanned
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:794:# here too when they exist -- they are the newer, less-exercised rigs, which is exactly
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:795:# where a distress marker is most likely to be informative.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:810:# ---- markers with a KNOWN NON-ZERO baseline ----------------------------
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:811:# '{ asc: data in, lenIn=.. lenIn2=.. }' (dev_asc.c:417) fires whenever a SCSI target has
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:812:# fewer bytes ready than the guest asked for. That is an ordinary occurrence -- measured
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:813:# at 1 on pmax and 5 on arc across a healthy OpenBSD 2.2 boot -- so demanding zero was
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:814:# simply wrong, and this gate failed on it the first time it ran.
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:815:#
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:816:# It is still worth watching, because the failure mode that matters is a FLOOD: round 40
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:817:# found #265's own diagnostic firing on every healthy boot, and that showed up only as a
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:818:# marker count. So the assertion is a CEILING rather than an equality -- generous enough
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:819:# that ordinary boot-to-boot variation cannot trip it, tight enough that a per-transfer
regress/gate_hygiene.sh:820:# flood will.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1:#!/bin/bash
regress/gate_offline.sh:2:# GATE 2 -- offline differential of the REAL ieee_store_float_value().
regress/gate_offline.sh:3:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:4:# The strongest gate here, and the cheapest, but only because it links the shipped
regress/gate_offline.sh:5:# src/core/float_emul.c. The first version of this gate transcribed BOTH sides of the
regress/gate_offline.sh:6:# differential and compared the copy against itself -- it never compiled or executed
regress/gate_offline.sh:7:# float_emul.c at all, so deleting #287 from the shipped source left it green. Review
regress/gate_offline.sh:8:# caught that, not the gate. Two things now prevent a repeat:
regress/gate_offline.sh:9:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:10:#   * the real file is compiled and linked into the driver, and
regress/gate_offline.sh:11:#   * the file compiled is asserted byte-identical to the committed one, so "the test
regress/gate_offline.sh:12:#     passed" and "the repository is correct" are the same statement.
regress/gate_offline.sh:13:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:14:# misc.h includes ../../config.h, which configure generates, so the compile has to happen
regress/gate_offline.sh:15:# against a CONFIGURED tree. That tree is a copy of the committed source (no VPATH in this
regress/gate_offline.sh:16:# project), which is exactly why the identity check above is required rather than assumed.
regress/gate_offline.sh:21:SEC=$ROOT/GXEMUL-SEC
regress/gate_offline.sh:26:# #375: the mips*_loadstore[32] dispatch-table index is a SILENT CROSS-FILE
regress/gate_offline.sh:27:# COUPLING. generate_mips_loadstore.c emits the table in the loop order
regress/gate_offline.sh:28:# `endianness -> store -> size -> signedness`, so an entry's index is
regress/gate_offline.sh:29:# endianness*16 + store*8 + size*2 + signedness. That arithmetic is then
regress/gate_offline.sh:30:# hard-coded BY HAND, with no comment and no check, at a dozen sites: the
regress/gate_offline.sh:31:# multi-transfer fold's bail (`mips32_loadstore[5]` = plain lw), the coproc
regress/gate_offline.sh:32:# handlers (lwc1->5, swc1->12, ldc1->7, sdc1->14), the canonical decoder, and
regress/gate_offline.sh:33:# the COMBINE(nop)/strlen/#169 matcher slots. Reorder that one generator loop
regress/gate_offline.sh:34:# and every hand-coded index silently mis-resolves -- the fold bail dispatches
regress/gate_offline.sh:35:# the wrong access SIZE, ldc1 becomes lw -- and NOTHING fails to compile. These
regress/gate_offline.sh:36:# rows turn the coupling into a checked invariant, offline, needing no compiler
regress/gate_offline.sh:37:# and no rig (only the committed generated table). Found by the #46 MIPS-combiner
regress/gate_offline.sh:38:# audit; both boot rigs are LE, so the _be entries are dead code on the harness
regress/gate_offline.sh:39:# but the coupling is real for any future BE MIPS work.
regress/gate_offline.sh:40:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:41:# The byte rows are load-bearing in the opposite direction: size==0 has no
regress/gate_offline.sh:42:# endianness, so the generator makes the byte LE and BE entries the SAME symbol
regress/gate_offline.sh:43:# ([1]==[17], [8]==[24]). #169's byte-store check and the strlen byte-load check
regress/gate_offline.sh:44:# rely on that share; the word rows ([5]!=[21]) prove the discrimination the byte
regress/gate_offline.sh:45:# rows deliberately lack. If a generator change ever split the byte entries, the
regress/gate_offline.sh:46:# `|| [x+16]` matcher tautologies become live and those checks silently weaken.
regress/gate_offline.sh:50:#  ls_entry <array-name> <index> -> the function symbol at that index, read from
regress/gate_offline.sh:51:#  the committed generated table (the [32] arrays, not the [16] generic ones).
regress/gate_offline.sh:74:#  b1w: the pty record parser's selftest, and a POSITIVE CONTROL that proves the selftest can
regress/gate_offline.sh:75:#  still catch something.  Placed BEFORE the compiler check on purpose: it needs only python3,
regress/gate_offline.sh:76:#  and a machine without cc must not silently lose parser coverage as well as the
regress/gate_offline.sh:77:#  differentials.  A measured census (absorb_census.py, 22 mutants) is what set the row floor;
regress/gate_offline.sh:78:#  the shipped selftest before this round killed 17 of those 22 while reporting every row
regress/gate_offline.sh:79:#  green, because its rows compared body+tail and a mutant that only MOVES text between the
regress/gate_offline.sh:80:#  halves preserves the sum.
regress/gate_offline.sh:88:#  Name the two rows the census showed were load-bearing, so deleting either is a red row
regress/gate_offline.sh:89:#  rather than a quieter file.  (#410's lesson: a row that can only be counted cannot be
regress/gate_offline.sh:90:#  missed by name.)
regress/gate_offline.sh:93:#  A FLOOR: three today, and adding a fourth guest-text fixture must not red the gate.
regress/gate_offline.sh:97:#  #425/#426: the budget the driver PRINTS must be the budget it ENFORCES, and the gate's
regress/gate_offline.sh:98:#  budget classifier must be exercisable without a rig.  Both are offline.
regress/gate_offline.sh:99:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:100:#  The straddle (selftest_budget.py) reads BUDGET=N from the driver's OWN OUTPUT and then
regress/gate_offline.sh:101:#  runs one leg under N and one over it.  MEASURED against three mutants in copies: a
regress/gate_offline.sh:102:#  call-site multiply, a multiply at the COMPARISON, and a deleted stop -- all three go red,
regress/gate_offline.sh:103:#  and the unmutated copy passes.  The comparison mutant is the one NO printing scheme can
regress/gate_offline.sh:104:#  catch, which is why this row exists at all.
regress/gate_offline.sh:117:#  #467: the deadline must fail on SILENCE, not on ELAPSED TIME.  Every pty probe used to
regress/gate_offline.sh:118:#  spend a TOTAL-TIME budget, so a boot that took 90 s on a loaded host failed while bytes
regress/gate_offline.sh:119:#  were arriving throughout -- slow was indistinguishable from stuck, and the battery could
regress/gate_offline.sh:120:#  only be trusted on an idle machine.
regress/gate_offline.sh:121:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:122:#  THE TWO LEGS ARE OPPOSITE IN SIGN AND BOTH ARE CHECKED HERE.  A conversion that made the
regress/gate_offline.sh:123:#  timeout UNREACHABLE would pass the slow-but-progressing leg and defend nothing; the
regress/gate_offline.sh:124:#  genuinely-stuck leg is what refuses that.  Checking only the first would be the
regress/gate_offline.sh:125:#  check-that-cannot-fail shape this tree keeps finding in its own work.
regress/gate_offline.sh:132:#  budget_class lives in lib.sh precisely so this truth table can run here rather than only
regress/gate_offline.sh:133:#  under a weekly rig boot.  `zeroed` is the case that used to print a GREEN "deliberately
regress/gate_offline.sh:134:#  absent" for a rig whose budget is 12 G -- measured at 11/11 PASS before this round.
regress/gate_offline.sh:143:#  THE POSITIVE CONTROL IS THE NAMED SURVIVOR ITSELF, applied to COPIES -- never the repo.
regress/gate_offline.sh:144:#  An in-process wrong-expectation row would only prove the reporter can print FAIL; this
regress/gate_offline.sh:145:#  proves THE ROW KILLS THE MUTANT.  Three states stay distinguishable IN AGGREGATE: never
regress/gate_offline.sh:146:#  ran (empty log -- rows 1, 3 and 4 red; note that row 2 asks only for a nonzero status and
regress/gate_offline.sh:147:#  a never-run python3 also exits nonzero, so THAT ROW ALONE DOES NOT SEPARATE THEM, which a
regress/gate_offline.sh:148:#  review seat measured), ran and crashed (nonzero status, NO verdict token), ran and caught
regress/gate_offline.sh:149:#  (the token plus the killing row's name).  The gate is red in all three failure shapes.
regress/gate_offline.sh:159:    print("CONTROL_STALE applies %d times" % src.count(old)); sys.exit(1)
regress/gate_offline.sh:173:#  A FLOOR, not an exact count.  The mutant strands every bracket-carrying line in the tail,
regress/gate_offline.sh:174:#  so it fails EVERY "lands in BODY" row -- three today.  Pinning the exact number would turn
regress/gate_offline.sh:175:#  a legitimately added row into a red gate; what must be true is that the kill comes from
regress/gate_offline.sh:176:#  THAT NAMED ROW and not from some unrelated collapse.
regress/gate_offline.sh:179:#  KEEP THE LOGS WHEN THE CONTROL FAILS.  Removing them unconditionally deleted the only
regress/gate_offline.sh:180:#  evidence at exactly the moment it was needed.
regress/gate_offline.sh:184:    gate_skip "no C compiler on PATH"
regress/gate_offline.sh:188:# Pick a configured tree: it must have the generated config.h AND the header directory.
regress/gate_offline.sh:193:[ -n "$TREE" ] || gate_skip "no configured tree (run gate_build.sh first)"
regress/gate_offline.sh:196:# THE HONESTY LINK. Without this the gate tests whatever happens to be in a scratch
regress/gate_offline.sh:197:# directory, which may lag the repository by any amount.
regress/gate_offline.sh:206:# #299's 2Sum exactness contract is enforced by GLOBAL build facts, not by anything
regress/gate_offline.sh:207:# local to the helper: it needs strict IEEE doubles, so -ffast-math (or friends) in the
regress/gate_offline.sh:208:# TREE's configure would break the shipped emulator while this gate -- which compiles
regress/gate_offline.sh:209:# with its own flags -- stayed green. A panel seat named that exact hole, so the gate
regress/gate_offline.sh:210:# trips on the flags themselves.
regress/gate_offline.sh:218:# #303: the grep above reads the SCRIPTS; `CFLAGS=-Ofast ./configure` lands only in the
regress/gate_offline.sh:219:# GENERATED Makefiles, which the emulator is actually built from, while this gate compiles
regress/gate_offline.sh:220:# with its own -O2 -- a poisoned tree would misdecode D subnormals in the emulator with
regress/gate_offline.sh:221:# every offline check green. So grep what the build actually uses, in every configured
regress/gate_offline.sh:222:# tree present. This grep is the ONLY defence against tree-flag poisoning -- the runtime
regress/gate_offline.sh:223:# canary below runs in THIS gate's own -O2 binary and can only see HOST-level trouble
regress/gate_offline.sh:224:# (MXCSR FTZ/DAZ, a wrong rounding mode), a DIFFERENT hole (both were a panel seat's
regress/gate_offline.sh:225:# findings; a diff-review seat corrected the first version of this comment, which
regress/gate_offline.sh:226:# claimed the canary covered this one too).
regress/gate_offline.sh:237:BIN=$LOGDIR/diff_ieee_store
regress/gate_offline.sh:239:if ! $CC -O2 -I"$TREE/src/include" -o "$BIN" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:247:"$BIN" > "$LOG" 2>&1
regress/gate_offline.sh:253:# Absolute answers first: a differential alone is relative and passes when both sides are
regress/gate_offline.sh:254:# wrong the same way.
regress/gate_offline.sh:258:# NOTHING EXTRA CHANGED: no difference may appear outside the two expected groups.
regress/gate_offline.sh:261:# NOTHING WAS MISSED: everything inside those groups must actually differ. Without this
regress/gate_offline.sh:262:# half, the check only proves the differences it happened to see were allowed -- and a
regress/gate_offline.sh:263:# broken version that fixes overflow for negative numbers but not positive ones would
regress/gate_offline.sh:264:# sail through.
regress/gate_offline.sh:267:# #331: this was "D-format: change-set is empty" == 0, and its flip is the
regress/gate_offline.sh:268:# headline of that round -- upstream has NO gradual underflow in double
regress/gate_offline.sh:269:# precision (an empty "FP_SUBNORMAL: TODO" arm), so this tree now differs from
regress/gate_offline.sh:270:# it for every D subnormal it samples. The floor is what makes the assertion
regress/gate_offline.sh:271:# mean something: a bare "> 0" would be satisfied by a single surviving diff
regress/gate_offline.sh:272:# where the class is roughly ten thousand strong, which is the same
regress/gate_offline.sh:273:# cannot-fail species this gate's header warns about. Measured 9839 over the
regress/gate_offline.sh:274:# 20M-sample sweep (host-subnormal patterns are about 1 in 2048 of it).
regress/gate_offline.sh:281:# #331: was 2^128 -- the overflow threshold #287 fixed, which used to be the
regress/gate_offline.sh:282:# smallest input where the two implementations parted. The subnormal band is
regress/gate_offline.sh:283:# further down, so it now comes first. The overflow pins either side of it
regress/gate_offline.sh:284:# (clamp-at, exp255-at) are unchanged, which is what shows this moved because
regress/gate_offline.sh:285:# a NEW class appeared below it rather than because the old one drifted.
regress/gate_offline.sh:288:# #292: the mode-aware entry point, checked against INDEPENDENT oracles (the host's own
regress/gate_offline.sh:289:# correctly-rounded float conversion) rather than against upstream. Named vectors carry
regress/gate_offline.sh:290:# the cases a random sweep cannot hit -- an exact half-way tie occurs about once per 2^29
regress/gate_offline.sh:291:# random inputs.
regress/gate_offline.sh:298:# #303: the DECODE side. The canary is load-bearing for HOST-level trouble only --
regress/gate_offline.sh:299:# MXCSR FTZ/DAZ or a non-nearest rounding mode void every D-subnormal expectation, and
regress/gate_offline.sh:300:# the canary is the only check that can tell. (Tree-flag poisoning is the generated-
regress/gate_offline.sh:301:# Makefile grep's job above; this binary compiles with its own -O2 and cannot see tree
regress/gate_offline.sh:302:# flags.) It is computed with volatile operands at runtime -- a constant expression
regress/gate_offline.sh:303:# would fold at compile time and pass on exactly the build it exists to catch.
regress/gate_offline.sh:315:# ---- #392: the readiness predicate, proved offline ----------------------
regress/gate_offline.sh:316:# Belongs in THIS gate because it needs no emulator, no pty and no host timing --
regress/gate_offline.sh:317:# it replays the probes' wait() loop over a scripted byte stream. That property is
regress/gate_offline.sh:318:# the point: a readiness test whose verdict moved with host load would repeat the
regress/gate_offline.sh:319:# mistake that once false-FAILed a 45-minute battery.
regress/gate_offline.sh:320:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:321:# The four rows are a truth table, and the middle two are the interesting ones:
regress/gate_offline.sh:322:# changing the prompt STRING alone does not fix the defect (full+whole still
regress/gate_offline.sh:323:# returns after one byte), and anchoring alone does not either. Only both together
regress/gate_offline.sh:324:# read the reply. If a future change makes 'full-whole' pass, the predicate has
regress/gate_offline.sh:325:# stopped being tested rather than started working.
regress/gate_offline.sh:326:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:327:# NOTE what this does NOT prove: it tests the four FORMS, not the probes, so on
regress/gate_offline.sh:328:# its own it would stay green if a probe were reverted. The static census in
regress/gate_offline.sh:329:# gate_hygiene.sh is what binds the shipped code to this result.
regress/gate_offline.sh:330:#  A PER-RUN NONCE. It RAISES THE BAR AND DOES NOT CLOSE THE DOOR, and the
regress/gate_offline.sh:331:#  previous version of this comment claimed otherwise -- wrongly.
regress/gate_offline.sh:332:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:333:#  What it does stop: a static transcript. The nonce enters the scripted reply,
regress/gate_offline.sh:334:#  so the reported byte counts shift by exactly its length, and a file of frozen
regress/gate_offline.sh:335:#  numbers fails (measured: fake reports 53 where 59 is required).
regress/gate_offline.sh:336:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:337:#  *** WHAT IT DOES NOT STOP, MEASURED BY A SEAT: the nonce is passed as an
regress/gate_offline.sh:338:#  ARGUMENT TO THE VERY SCRIPT BEING VALIDATED, so a fake needs only
regress/gate_offline.sh:339:#  len(sys.argv[1]) to compute the affine counts. A 12-line transcript that runs
regress/gate_offline.sh:340:#  no loops passed all ten checks here and both pins in gate_hygiene. ***
regress/gate_offline.sh:341:#  An oracle that asks the prover to KNOW A PUBLIC INPUT is not an oracle that
regress/gate_offline.sh:342:#  asks it to DO THE WORK. A nonce only binds when it is WITHHELD -- the gate
regress/gate_offline.sh:343:#  would have to recompute the expected stream itself, or vary something whose
regress/gate_offline.sh:344:#  effect is not a length the script is handed. That is a design change, filed
regress/gate_offline.sh:345:#  rather than rushed; see the queue entry on binding this test to real work.
regress/gate_offline.sh:346:#  Recorded here rather than quietly weakened, because a check that overstates
regress/gate_offline.sh:347:#  what it proves is worse than one that states its limit.
regress/gate_offline.sh:348:#  d1: the driver must put BOTH pty logs where $LOGDIR says.  This cannot be a gate-5 row:
regress/gate_offline.sh:349:#  in the default battery $LOGDIR IS the string the driver used to hardcode, so such a row
regress/gate_offline.sh:350:#  passes on the unfixed code -- measured.  selftest_logdir.py points LOGDIR somewhere else
regress/gate_offline.sh:351:#  and runs a stub in place of the emulator, so it needs no rig and no boot.  It sets the
regress/gate_offline.sh:352:#  variable itself rather than being invoked as `env LOGDIR=... `, which would export from
regress/gate_offline.sh:353:#  OUTSIDE and stay green with lib.sh's export missing (half the defect).
regress/gate_offline.sh:371:#  The two LATE-PROMPT rows are the ECHO conjunct's only behavioural coverage.
regress/gate_offline.sh:372:#  A seat measured that the echo half could be deleted from all fourteen sites
regress/gate_offline.sh:373:#  with every gate still green, because the four rows above exercise the mark and
regress/gate_offline.sh:374:#  the prompt string and nothing exercised the echo. These two do: a stale prompt
regress/gate_offline.sh:375:#  arriving AFTER the mark cannot be distinguished by byte anchoring, only by
regress/gate_offline.sh:376:#  requiring the new command's own echo first.
regress/gate_offline.sh:379:#  THE ANTI-FAKE CHECK. Both counts are affine in the nonce length, so a printed
regress/gate_offline.sh:380:#  transcript OF FROZEN NUMBERS cannot satisfy them -- but one that reads the
regress/gate_offline.sh:381:#  nonce from argv can, which is why this is a bar and not a door (see above).
regress/gate_offline.sh:382:#  53 and 60 are the measured baselines at
regress/gate_offline.sh:383:#  nonce="" and the arithmetic was verified at lengths 0, 5 and 10.
regress/gate_offline.sh:388:# ---- #400: the SH-4 TMU tick arithmetic -----------------------------------------
regress/gate_offline.sh:389:# Same shape as the float_emul differential above, and for the same reason: the driver
regress/gate_offline.sh:390:# stubs fatal() and #includes dev_sh4.c, so the sh4_timer_tick() that runs IS the one
regress/gate_offline.sh:391:# that ships. sh4_timer_tick is static, so there is no way to link it without including
regress/gate_offline.sh:392:# the file -- which is precisely what makes a transcription impossible here.
regress/gate_offline.sh:393:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:394:# The rows are exact values, not "did it move". The defect being guarded pinned TCNT at
regress/gate_offline.sh:395:# zero permanently on a booting OpenBSD/landisk guest after 515.4 s; the no-freeze row
regress/gate_offline.sh:396:# reproduces that in milliseconds instead of a 9.5-minute boot.
regress/gate_offline.sh:397:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:398:# The three-timer row exists because a measure seat found two wrong variants -- tcor[0]
regress/gate_offline.sh:399:# for tcor[i], and cnt from tcnt[0] -- that are BIT-IDENTICAL to correct code across all
regress/gate_offline.sh:400:# 824288 single-timer cases, because every other row starts timer 0 only. They are the
regress/gate_offline.sh:401:# typo this rewrite newly risks. Measured: that row alone kills both.
regress/gate_offline.sh:402:#  #427-#429: the timer core's frequency domain and its catch-up bound, driven OFFLINE.
regress/gate_offline.sh:403:#  The driver #includes the shipped src/core/timer.c (timer_tick is static), stubs only
regress/gate_offline.sh:404:#  gettimeofday, and never installs a signal handler -- so no host clock enters and these
regress/gate_offline.sh:405:#  rows are not load-sensitive.  MEASURED against six mutants in copies: no ceiling,
regress/gate_offline.sh:406:#  clamp-below-the-early-out, unbounded catch-up, both-clamps-written-plainly, and the
regress/gate_offline.sh:407:#  drop-the-backlog policy all go red; the shipped file passes.
regress/gate_offline.sh:408:TIMBIN=$LOGDIR/diff_timer
regress/gate_offline.sh:409:TIMLOG=$LOGDIR/diff_timer.log
regress/gate_offline.sh:410:#  -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE, because strict c99 hides struct sigaction and SA_RESTART, which the
regress/gate_offline.sh:411:#  shipped timer.c uses -- the driver never calls that code, but it still has to compile.
regress/gate_offline.sh:413:        -o "$TIMBIN" "$HERE/diff_timer.c" -lm > "$TIMLOG" 2>&1; then
regress/gate_offline.sh:414:    note "timer differential compile failed:"; sed 's/^/       /' "$TIMLOG" | head -12
regress/gate_offline.sh:415:    check "timer: compiles against the real timer.c" "no" "yes"
regress/gate_offline.sh:417:    check "timer: compiles against the real timer.c" "yes" "yes"
regress/gate_offline.sh:418:    "$TIMBIN" > "$TIMLOG" 2>&1
regress/gate_offline.sh:420:    check     "timer: row failures" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:426:    check_min "timer: rows actually run" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:428:    check     "timer: offline verdict" "$(grep -c 'DIFF_TIMER_PASS' "$TIMLOG")" "1"
regress/gate_offline.sh:432:    check "timer: the NaN row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:434:    check "timer: the idempotence row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:436:    check "timer: the anti-regression rows are present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:440:    check "timer: the ceiling-is-INT_MAX row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:442:    check "timer: the floor-is-reachable row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:446:    check "timer: the 40 MHz keeps-time row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:447:          "$(grep -c 'a 40 MHz timer' "$TIMLOG")" "1"
regress/gate_offline.sh:449:    check "timer: the exact-bound row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:451:    check "timer: the backlog-retained row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:455:    check "timer: the stall-is-real row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:459:    check "timer: the cap-is-reported row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:461:    check "timer: the complaint-is-made row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:463:    check "timer: the interval-is-reciprocal row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:467:#  #430-#431: memory_rw() translated the START vaddr once and then copied the whole length
regress/gate_offline.sh:468:#  from that page's host pointer, so a page-crossing access walked PHYSICALLY contiguous
regress/gate_offline.sh:469:#  host bytes across a VIRTUALLY contiguous guest span.  Driven OFFLINE, because the defect
regress/gate_offline.sh:470:#  needs two pages adjacent virtually and NOT adjacent physically and no rig in this tree can
regress/gate_offline.sh:471:#  be made to produce that on demand -- the map comes from a stub instead.
regress/gate_offline.sh:472:#  MEASURED against the pre-fix file (git show b9daca1:src/cpus/memory_rw.c): 11 of these
regress/gate_offline.sh:473:#  rows go red, including the reproduction (a straddling read returning the physically-
regress/gate_offline.sh:474:#  following page), the straddling WRITE corrupting an unrelated page, the device case, and
regress/gate_offline.sh:475:#  the missing fault.  -I"$SEC/src" because the driver includes "include/misc.h" the way the
regress/gate_offline.sh:476:#  cpu files do.
regress/gate_offline.sh:477:MRWBIN=$LOGDIR/diff_memory_rw
regress/gate_offline.sh:479:#  -fno-optimize-sibling-calls IS LOAD-BEARING, and only for the stack row.  gcc 15.2.1
regress/gate_offline.sh:480:#  DOES eliminate the tail call at -O2, so a recursion mutant measured a 96-byte span and
regress/gate_offline.sh:481:#  the "it is a loop" row passed under the very defect it exists to catch -- vacuous.
regress/gate_offline.sh:482:#  With the flag: loop 0 bytes, recursion 1,572,768.  Measured both ways by a review seat.
regress/gate_offline.sh:485:        -o "$MRWBIN" "$HERE/diff_memory_rw.c" -lm > "$MRWLOG" 2>&1; then
regress/gate_offline.sh:490:    "$MRWBIN" > "$MRWLOG" 2>&1
regress/gate_offline.sh:496:    #  had to fix in the timer differential.
regress/gate_offline.sh:512:    #  absorb 52, budget 7, timer 24, memory_rw 23 (this one, after the fix), footbridge 19,
regress/gate_offline.sh:549:#  #432: the footbridge timer divided by a guest-written zero -- a guest-reachable HOST CRASH,
regress/gate_offline.sh:550:#  reproduced independently by two seats.  Driven OFFLINE because THERE IS NO NETWINDER OR CATS
regress/gate_offline.sh:551:#  RIG ANYWHERE IN THIS TREE: nothing here boots a machine that has a footbridge, so this is not
regress/gate_offline.sh:552:#  the cheapest detector, it is the only possible one.  The driver #includes the shipped device,
regress/gate_offline.sh:553:#  exactly as diff_sh4_tmu.c does.
regress/gate_offline.sh:554:#  -Wl,--gc-sections is LOAD-BEARING: without it the link needs eleven more symbols.  The
regress/gate_offline.sh:555:#  trade-off, stated so it is not mistaken for coverage: it drops DEVINIT, so this file cannot
regress/gate_offline.sh:556:#  test devinit.  -D_DEFAULT_SOURCE is required for random() under -std=c99.
regress/gate_offline.sh:557:#  MEASURED against seven mutants in copies -- all red, control passes: the zero guard removed
regress/gate_offline.sh:558:#  (dies BY SIGNAL 8, the crash itself), the prescaler back to a signed shift, the tick site
regress/gate_offline.sh:559:#  prescaling, TIMER_EXTERNAL falling through as a bare x16, and the three that PASS 2 found
regress/gate_offline.sh:560:#  surviving the first fifteen rows -- one character deleting the prescaler from the RATE domain,
regress/gate_offline.sh:561:#  the tick counter replaced by a constant, and normalize-at-write (the design this round
regress/gate_offline.sh:562:#  REJECTED).  An eighth is EQUIVALENT and deliberately not counted: on a uint64_t, `<<= 4` and
regress/gate_offline.sh:563:#  `*= 16` are the same operation for every value in range -- it is the TYPE that makes it
regress/gate_offline.sh:564:#  equivalent, not the magnitude.
regress/gate_offline.sh:565:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:566:#  ON THE FORKS, CORRECTED: two rows fork, and the first version of this comment said that made
regress/gate_offline.sh:567:#  a re-crashing mutant a named-row KILL rather than a census FAULT.  MEASURED FALSE by two seats
regress/gate_offline.sh:568:#  independently -- D2 is UNFORKED and reaches the divide first, so an unconditional
regress/gate_offline.sh:569:#  re-introduction of the crash takes the binary down there, before F1's fork.  G1's fork IS
regress/gate_offline.sh:570:#  load-bearing (the CONTROL-arm exit(1) is reachable nowhere else in-process) and F1's contains
regress/gate_offline.sh:571:#  a narrower class (a zero-guard conditioned on the prescaler bits survives D2 and dies in F1).
regress/gate_offline.sh:572:#  Detection is unaffected either way: every grep below fails on a truncated log.
regress/gate_offline.sh:573:FBBIN=$LOGDIR/diff_footbridge
regress/gate_offline.sh:578:        -o "$FBBIN" "$HERE/diff_footbridge.c" -lm > "$FBLOG" 2>&1; then
regress/gate_offline.sh:583:    "$FBBIN" > "$FBLOG" 2>&1
regress/gate_offline.sh:612:    #  Sibling sweep at the time of the fix: 5 bracket patterns among 175
regress/gate_offline.sh:626:    #    TIMER_ENABLE   a DISABLED timer can never hold a pending interrupt, so a guard
regress/gate_offline.sh:643:          "$(grep -c 'an ENABLED timer READ does not clear' "$FBLOG")" "1"
regress/gate_offline.sh:647:          "$(grep -c '0x32C decrements timer index 1' "$FBLOG")" "1"
regress/gate_offline.sh:649:          "$(grep -c '0x36C decrements timer index 3' "$FBLOG")" "1"
regress/gate_offline.sh:674:#  #433: the M8820x CMMU terminated the HOST process on an ordinary guest register access --
regress/gate_offline.sh:675:#  measured at 1007 of 1024 word offsets on a plain READ, on luna88k, which BOOTS in this tree.
regress/gate_offline.sh:676:#  Driven offline for BREADTH (1024 offsets x 2 directions x 256 commands is not a pty job);
regress/gate_offline.sh:677:#  the online cold-debugger probe that proves guest-instruction reachability is filed separately.
regress/gate_offline.sh:678:#  THE KILL CRITERION IS THE EXIT STATUS, not "the child died": the first reproduction of this
regress/gate_offline.sh:679:#  defect overcounted by exactly six in each direction because six offsets crashed in the
regress/gate_offline.sh:680:#  HARNESS (the register-file arm invalidates before the writeflag check, so it fires on reads,
regress/gate_offline.sh:681:#  and the driver had left that callback NULL).  A signal death is reported as a FAULT here,
regress/gate_offline.sh:682:#  never counted as a detection.
regress/gate_offline.sh:683:M8BIN=$LOGDIR/diff_m8820x
regress/gate_offline.sh:688:        -o "$M8BIN" "$HERE/diff_m8820x.c" -lm > "$M8LOG" 2>&1; then
regress/gate_offline.sh:693:    "$M8BIN" > "$M8LOG" 2>&1
regress/gate_offline.sh:710:    #  exercises 640,016 times per boot.
regress/gate_offline.sh:741:#  #434 -- a guest READ of six CMMU registers purged the whole translation cache.
regress/gate_offline.sh:742:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:743:#  THE ROWS THAT MATTER HERE ARE THE ARGUMENT ROWS, and that is not a stylistic
regress/gate_offline.sh:744:#  preference.  A measuring seat built the one-identifier mutant
regress/gate_offline.sh:745:#  INVALIDATE_ALL -> INVALIDATE_VADDR as a real emulator and BOOTED THE luna88k RIG
regress/gate_offline.sh:746:#  WITH IT: 1:1:1 to `login:`, no diagnostics.  The purge is gutted to virtual page
regress/gate_offline.sh:747:#  0 -- under-invalidation, the dangerous direction -- while the CALL COUNT stays
regress/gate_offline.sh:748:#  bit-identical.  A count-based offline row cannot catch it, and THAT THREE-RUN
regress/gate_offline.sh:749:#  BOOT-TO-LOGIN TRIAL did not; a different workload or assertion on the same rig
regress/gate_offline.sh:750:#  might.  The likely masking mechanism is that the guest issues ~603,000 SCR
regress/gate_offline.sh:751:#  flushes per boot, each doing its own INVALIDATE_ALL -- BELIEVED, since a
regress/gate_offline.sh:752:#  successful boot alongside a plausible mechanism is not established attribution.
regress/gate_offline.sh:753:#  ARGUMENT-INSPECTING ROWS are what catch it, F1 AND F2 both, which is why the
regress/gate_offline.sh:754:#  named checks below pin the ARGUMENT rows and the cpu/cmmu rows and not the
regress/gate_offline.sh:755:#  totals.
regress/gate_offline.sh:756:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:757:#  *** THESE THREE SENTENCES WERE RETRACTED IN diff_m8invread.c AND SURVIVED HERE
regress/gate_offline.sh:758:#  VERBATIM FOR ONE PASS. ***  A pass-2 seat grepped and found each phrase alive in
regress/gate_offline.sh:759:#  exactly one file -- this one.  That is the project's own "when correcting a
regress/gate_offline.sh:760:#  claim, grep for its siblings" rule failing on its first application, in the very
regress/gate_offline.sh:761:#  round that wrote the corrections.  A retraction that lives in one file is half a
regress/gate_offline.sh:762:#  retraction.
regress/gate_offline.sh:763:IVBIN=$LOGDIR/diff_m8invread
regress/gate_offline.sh:765:#  WHICH FLAGS ARE ACTUALLY LOAD-BEARING, ABLATED ONE AT A TIME RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
regress/gate_offline.sh:766:#  The first version of this comment said "-I$SEC/src and -lm are BOTH load-bearing";
regress/gate_offline.sh:767:#  a pass-2 seat compiled all four variants and measured:
regress/gate_offline.sh:768:#      -I"$SEC/src"          LOAD-BEARING -- without it, `fatal error: include/misc.h`
regress/gate_offline.sh:769:#      -Wl,--gc-sections     LOAD-BEARING -- without it, `undefined reference to
regress/gate_offline.sh:770:#                            memory_device_register` from DEVINIT(m8820x).  It was NOT
regress/gate_offline.sh:771:#                            named, which is the worst case: the one nobody knew about.
regress/gate_offline.sh:772:#      -lm                   NOT load-bearing -- links and passes 21/21 without it.
regress/gate_offline.sh:773:#  The command was right and the explanation was wrong, which is the failure mode a
regress/gate_offline.sh:774:#  comment has that code does not.  Fitting, since this block's whole point is a lesson
regress/gate_offline.sh:775:#  about copying a neighbouring block: it was copied from the SH-4 TMU block, whose
regress/gate_offline.sh:776:#  differential includes nothing from src/, and the gate caught THAT loudly -- a compile
regress/gate_offline.sh:777:#  failure is a FAIL here, never a skip.
regress/gate_offline.sh:778:if ! $CC -O2 -std=c99 -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L -D_DEFAULT_SOURCE         -I"$SEC/src" -I"$SEC/src/include" -I"$SEC/src/include/thirdparty"         -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -Wl,--gc-sections         -o "$IVBIN" "$HERE/diff_m8invread.c" -lm > "$IVLOG" 2>&1; then
regress/gate_offline.sh:783:    "$IVBIN" > "$IVLOG" 2>&1
regress/gate_offline.sh:852:# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
regress/gate_offline.sh:853:#  CAN THESE DETECTORS STILL FAIL?  (#436)
regress/gate_offline.sh:854:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:855:#  Gate 3 is the battery's only failability control and it mutates ONE file,
regress/gate_offline.sh:856:#  src/core/float_emul.c.  Eleven differentials live here; gate 3 names one.  A
regress/gate_offline.sh:857:#  measuring seat reproduced the consequence: stub check() so it never compares,
regress/gate_offline.sh:858:#  and 118 rows across five detectors print `ok`, keep their row counts, keep
regress/gate_offline.sh:859:#  their identity rows, keep their verdict tokens -- and detect NOTHING, with
regress/gate_offline.sh:860:#  every assertion in this file green.
regress/gate_offline.sh:861:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:862:#  The reason was structural, not an oversight in any one row: every assertion in
regress/gate_offline.sh:863:#  this file grepped the detector's own stdout, and all of that stdout is downstream
regress/gate_offline.sh:864:#  of the single comparison the stub removes.  They were ONE EQUIVALENCE CLASS, and
regress/gate_offline.sh:865:#  the stub stepped over all of it at once.
regress/gate_offline.sh:866:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:867:#  PAST TENSE ON PURPOSE.  A pass-2 seat pointed out that this sentence was written
regress/gate_offline.sh:868:#  in the PRESENT tense by the very hunk that falsified it: the five selfmutant_one
regress/gate_offline.sh:869:#  checks grep the HELPER's output (varied input) and the manifest checks grep the
regress/gate_offline.sh:870:#  FILESYSTEM, so a handful of this gate's assertions are now outside the class.
regress/gate_offline.sh:871:#  That is the whole point of adding them, and stating it as a present fact would
regress/gate_offline.sh:872:#  have made the comment wrong the moment it landed.  Only a check that VARIES THE INPUT and
regress/gate_offline.sh:873:#  demands the output track it escapes.
regress/gate_offline.sh:874:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:875:#  TWO CONTROLS, COVERING DIFFERENT FAILURES, NEITHER SUBSTITUTING FOR THE OTHER:
regress/gate_offline.sh:876:#    * the in-detector SELFCHECK row (in the .c files) -- feeds check() a
regress/gate_offline.sh:877:#      mismatch and requires the failure counter to move.  Covers EVERY row in
regress/gate_offline.sh:878:#      its file against the comparator dying.  Free, no extra compile.
regress/gate_offline.sh:879:#    * the self-mutants below -- break the code the detector watches and require
regress/gate_offline.sh:880:#      the NAMED row to catch it.  Covers ONE site against a fixture that has
regress/gate_offline.sh:881:#      stopped reaching the code.  Measured: the SELFCHECK is blind to a row
regress/gate_offline.sh:882:#      rewritten to compare the subject against itself; a pinned mutant is not.
regress/gate_offline.sh:883:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:884:#  WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROVE, said plainly because the honest scope is the whole
regress/gate_offline.sh:885:#  point.  A seat stubbed every row EXCEPT the ones each mutant kills and both
regress/gate_offline.sh:886:#  this gate and the self-mutant stayed green -- 23 of 24 rows inert for timer,
regress/gate_offline.sh:887:#  25 of 27 for m8invread.  So this is a LIVENESS CONTROL: it proves the detector
regress/gate_offline.sh:888:#  is not wholly inert and that one named row still fires.  It is not a census
regress/gate_offline.sh:889:#  and must never be read as "the detector is fine".  Going from 0 to 1 is not
regress/gate_offline.sh:890:#  false comfort; CALLING 1 "the detector works" is.
regress/gate_offline.sh:891:selfmutant_one() {   # harness subject stem row-id [extra-cc-flags]
regress/gate_offline.sh:910:#  *** F1: THE SELFCHECK ROW NEEDED A PRESENCE CHECK, AND NOT HAVING ONE WAS THE WORST
regress/gate_offline.sh:911:#  DEFECT IN THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS ROUND. ***  A pass-2 seat deleted the whole SELFCHECK
regress/gate_offline.sh:912:#  block from all five detectors and measured: gate 2 stayed PASS at the SAME check count with
regress/gate_offline.sh:913:#  ZERO red rows, byte-identical to baseline, and all five self-mutants still OK.
regress/gate_offline.sh:914:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:915:#  It is unprotected PRECISELY BECAUSE THE BOOKKEEPING IS CORRECT: the row restores `rows`, so
regress/gate_offline.sh:916:#  it is invisible to the only mechanism this gate has for noticing a deleted row -- the
regress/gate_offline.sh:917:#  `rows actually run` floor.  A few hundred lines above, this file already says "Name each row
regress/gate_offline.sh:918:#  that is the SOLE detector of something, so deleting one is a red row rather than a quieter
regress/gate_offline.sh:919:#  file."  The SELFCHECK row is the sole detector of comparator death in five files and was
regress/gate_offline.sh:920:#  named nowhere.  The doctrine was written down and then not applied to the next row added.
regress/gate_offline.sh:921:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:922:#  *** THE LIST THAT STOOD HERE WAS FIVE HARDCODED NAMES WITH NO MANIFEST BEHIND IT, and it
regress/gate_offline.sh:923:#  has moved to the end of this file (selfcheck7). ***  It asserted that five detectors ran
regress/gate_offline.sh:924:#  their sentinel and said NOTHING AT ALL about the other seven differentials, so a
regress/gate_offline.sh:925:#  differential arriving without a comparator control was declared nowhere -- the same
regress/gate_offline.sh:926:#  G-with-no-H shape the selfmutant manifest below was written to close, in this same file,
regress/gate_offline.sh:927:#  a few hundred lines apart.
regress/gate_offline.sh:928:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:929:#  IT COULD NOT BE FIXED IN PLACE, AND THE REASON IS A STALE-ARTEFACT TRAP RATHER THAN
regress/gate_offline.sh:930:#  TIDINESS.  Six of the twelve differentials do not compile or run until several hundred
regress/gate_offline.sh:931:#  lines further down, and $LOGDIR PERSISTS BETWEEN RUNS: a manifest-driven grep of
regress/gate_offline.sh:932:#  diff_sh4_tmu.log FROM HERE would read the PREVIOUS run's log and report a sentinel this
regress/gate_offline.sh:933:#  run never executed.  That is "regenerate before grepping a generated file" wearing
regress/gate_offline.sh:934:#  different clothes.  The whole control now lives in the SC_COVERED block just above
regress/gate_offline.sh:935:#  gate_end, where every log is this run's.
regress/gate_offline.sh:937:selfmutant_one diff_timer.c      src/core/timer.c            timer      "NaN"
regress/gate_offline.sh:938:selfmutant_one diff_memory_rw.c  src/cpus/memory_rw.c        memory_rw  "G1 " "-fno-optimize-sibling-calls"
regress/gate_offline.sh:939:selfmutant_one diff_footbridge.c src/devices/dev_footbridge.c footbridge "A1 "
regress/gate_offline.sh:940:selfmutant_one diff_m8820x.c     src/devices/dev_m8820x.c    m8820x     "D3"
regress/gate_offline.sh:941:selfmutant_one diff_m8invread.c  src/devices/dev_m8820x.c    m8invread  "F1 "
regress/gate_offline.sh:942:#  #437.  The mutant drops the fflush of the OVERLAY DATA file and KEEPS the fsync --
regress/gate_offline.sh:943:#  chosen over the obvious whole-function revert for a measured reason: it leaves the
regress/gate_offline.sh:944:#  fsync count at 2, so any test counting fsync alone stays GREEN, and only the fflush
regress/gate_offline.sh:945:#  row goes red (1, want 2).  It is also the edit a maintainer might make in good faith,
regress/gate_offline.sh:946:#  since "fsync already flushes, the fflush is redundant" is the reasoning that produced
regress/gate_offline.sh:947:#  the original defect -- which diskimage.c:1207-1211 exists to refute.  The --wrap flags
regress/gate_offline.sh:948:#  are load-bearing here, but NOT in the way -fno-optimize-sibling-calls is for memory_rw,
regress/gate_offline.sh:949:#  and the difference matters.  memory_rw's flag makes BOTH arms consistently wrong, so the
regress/gate_offline.sh:950:#  control cannot see it.  Here, omitting the flags makes the pristine arm fail to PASS, so
regress/gate_offline.sh:951:#  selfmutant.py reports SELFMUTANT_SETUP -- which its own header says is NEVER scored as a
regress/gate_offline.sh:952:#  detection.  Measured both ways.  The earlier comment asserted the memory_rw shape and
regress/gate_offline.sh:953:#  taught the wrong hazard class.
regress/gate_offline.sh:954:selfmutant_one diff_diskimage_sync.c src/disk/diskimage.c   diskimage_sync "overlay: sync flushes+syncs data" "-Wl,--wrap=fsync -Wl,--wrap=fflush"
regress/gate_offline.sh:955:#  selfmutant6, part 1 of 3.  Both mutants were chosen so that exactly ONE row sees them --
regress/gate_offline.sh:956:#  measured, 1 of 37 and 1 of 16 -- which is the diskimage_sync criterion: a mutant that
regress/gate_offline.sh:957:#  reddens five rows proves little beyond "deleting code breaks tests".
regress/gate_offline.sh:958:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:959:#  diskimage_parse drops `&& !prefix_s` from #418's escape-hatch guard.  THIS IS THE SEVENTH
regress/gate_offline.sh:960:#  MUTANT OF #418 PASS 2: a reading seat proposed it, it was executed, and it SURVIVED all 35
regress/gate_offline.sh:961:#  rows -- the row it now dies on was added to kill it.  On every rig this project boots the
regress/gate_offline.sh:962:#  term genuinely is redundant, because get_default_disk_type_for_machine() returns SCSI for
regress/gate_offline.sh:963:#  PMAX/ARC/SGI/LUNA88K/MVME88K, so `sd:` resolves to SCSI either way.  It bites only on an
regress/gate_offline.sh:964:#  IDE-default machine -- which is exactly what makes "this term is redundant" a plausible
regress/gate_offline.sh:965:#  good-faith edit rather than sabotage.
regress/gate_offline.sh:966:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:967:#  -std=gnu99 is the memory_rw hazard class, found by reading the compile lines rather than
regress/gate_offline.sh:968:#  by being bitten: gate_offline.sh builds this harness with -std=gnu99 while selfmutant.py's
regress/gate_offline.sh:969:#  hardcoded CC uses -std=c99, and EXTRA_FLAGS is appended so the trailing one wins.  MEASURED
regress/gate_offline.sh:970:#  green BOTH ways, so it is belt-and-braces -- but it makes the control compile what the
regress/gate_offline.sh:971:#  battery compiles, which is the entire reason that flag argument exists.
regress/gate_offline.sh:972:selfmutant_one diff_diskimage_parse.c src/disk/diskimage.c    diskimage_parse "'sd:' stays SCSI" "-std=gnu99"
regress/gate_offline.sh:973:#  wdc_identify zeroes word 6's high byte on the premise that sectors-per-track is an 8-bit
regress/gate_offline.sh:974:#  field.  THIS IS THE MUTANT #410 MEASURED SURVIVING AT ALL FIVE OPTIMISATION LEVELS: #409
regress/gate_offline.sh:975:#  shipped row_wide_geometry with s=17, so word 6's high byte was permanently zero while the
regress/gate_offline.sh:976:#  row's name claimed words 3 AND 6.  The maintainer's premise is false for the reason
regress/gate_offline.sh:977:#  dev_wdc.c's own bucket (b) gives -- `-d f:` yields spt 256 and 1280.  Deliberately NOT the
regress/gate_offline.sh:978:#  `% 255` revert, which reddens seven rows.
regress/gate_offline.sh:979:selfmutant_one diff_wdc_identify.c    src/devices/dev_wdc.c   wdc_identify    "geometry words carry their high byte"
regress/gate_offline.sh:980:#  selfmutant6, part 2.  THE I/O AND GEOMETRY DIFFERENTIALS, 79 rows between them.
regress/gate_offline.sh:981:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:982:#  *** BOTH NEEDED A VERDICT TOKEN BEFORE THEY COULD BE CONTROLLED AT ALL, and that is a
regress/gate_offline.sh:983:#  FINDING rather than a chore. ***  selfmutant.py asserts the FAIL token is PRESENT --
regress/gate_offline.sh:984:#  never that PASS is absent -- so a differential printing only a row table can be broken
regress/gate_offline.sh:985:#  in every row without the helper being able to say so.  diff_diskimage_geom.c printed
regress/gate_offline.sh:986:#  DISKIMAGE_GEOM_PASS and NOTHING on failure; diff_diskimage_io.c printed no token in
regress/gate_offline.sh:987:#  either direction.  MEASURED with the tokens missing: geom's mutant arm reported
regress/gate_offline.sh:988:#  SELFMUTANT_FAIL, "the detector did NOT report a failure", while its named row was in
regress/gate_offline.sh:989:#  fact RED -- the helper blaming the detector for an apparatus gap -- and io could not
regress/gate_offline.sh:990:#  get past its pristine arm at all (SELFMUTANT_SETUP).
regress/gate_offline.sh:991:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:992:#  I/O.  The mutant drops the READ-SIDE ZERO-FILL from #416's bound and KEEPS the bound,
regress/gate_offline.sh:993:#  so the 43 rows that assert "the access was refused" all stay GREEN and only the two
regress/gate_offline.sh:994:#  that assert what happens to the CALLER'S BYTES go red (MEASURED: 45 rows / 0 failures
regress/gate_offline.sh:995:#  pristine, 45 / 2 mutant).  Every mutant of the bound ITSELF -- advertised capacity
regress/gate_offline.sh:996:#  instead of the backed extent, a start-only bound, dropping `offset < 0` -- reddens four
regress/gate_offline.sh:997:#  to six rows across three sections and proves only that deleting a bound breaks the rows
regress/gate_offline.sh:998:#  written for it.  diskimage.c:1469-1473 exists to refute this mutant's premise ("we
regress/gate_offline.sh:999:#  return failure, so the caller must not look at the buffer"): dev_wdc's read path
regress/gate_offline.sh:1000:#  discards this function's return value and copies a 32 KB STACK buffer to the guest.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1001:#  -std=gnu99 is the memory_rw hazard class again -- MEASURED green both ways, supplied so
regress/gate_offline.sh:1002:#  the lane compiles what the battery compiles rather than something adjacent to it.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1003:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1004:#  The one-row alternative that was measured and NOT taken is recorded in the .why file:
regress/gate_offline.sh:1005:#  `lendone < (ssize_t)len` -> `lendone <= 0` kills exactly one row, but its kill depends
regress/gate_offline.sh:1006:#  on RLIMIT_FSIZE producing a genuine PARTIAL write, so on a host that absorbs 0 bytes
regress/gate_offline.sh:1007:#  instead of 256 the mutant survives and the control goes red for an environmental
regress/gate_offline.sh:1008:#  reason that reads exactly like a dead detector.  gate_ab's wall-clock oracle in
regress/gate_offline.sh:1009:#  miniature; determinism was preferred to a one-row pin.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1010:selfmutant_one diff_diskimage_io.c    src/disk/diskimage.c   diskimage_io   "refused read leaves NO caller bytes" "-std=gnu99"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1011:#  GEOMETRY.  strtoll -> strtoull at the SPT parse site ONLY; the heads field five lines
regress/gate_offline.sh:1012:#  above keeps strtoll, which is what makes it a plausible half-done edit rather than a
regress/gate_offline.sh:1013:#  rewrite.  strtoull applies a leading minus in UNSIGNED arithmetic, so
regress/gate_offline.sh:1014:#  `-d g16;-18446744073709551615:` folds to spt=1 and is ACCEPTED.  The premise -- "heads
regress/gate_offline.sh:1015:#  and sectors per track are counts, they cannot be negative, use the unsigned parse" --
regress/gate_offline.sh:1016:#  is the good-faith kind.  diff_diskimage_geom.c's own FAMILY C note (:605) records that
regress/gate_offline.sh:1017:#  this passed all 30 rows of that file's previous version.  MEASURED here: 34 rows / 0
regress/gate_offline.sh:1018:#  failures pristine, 34 / 1 mutant -- one red row, and it is the pinned one.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1019:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1020:#  *** THE EXTRA ARGUMENT IS A SOURCE FILE, NOT A FLAG, AND IT IS A THIRD HAZARD CLASS
regress/gate_offline.sh:1021:#  THAT NEITHER OF THE TWO ALREADY RECORDED IN THIS FILE COVERS. ***  Alone among these
regress/gate_offline.sh:1022:#  differentials, geom does NOT #include its subject: the battery compiles it against
regress/gate_offline.sh:1023:#  "$SEC/src/disk/diskimage.c" as a second translation unit, so the helper must be told to
regress/gate_offline.sh:1024:#  do the same, and the path must be RELATIVE because it is resolved from the mutant
regress/gate_offline.sh:1025:#  tree's own regress/ directory.  MEASURED all three ways:
regress/gate_offline.sh:1026:#    * omitted            -> link error -> SELFMUTANT_SETUP.  The diskimage_sync class:
regress/gate_offline.sh:1027:#                            the pristine arm cannot PASS, and SETUP is never a detection.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1028:#    * ABSOLUTE $SEC path -> the PRISTINE subject is compiled into BOTH arms, the mutant
regress/gate_offline.sh:1029:#                            survives, and the helper prints SELFMUTANT_FAIL -- red, but
regress/gate_offline.sh:1030:#                            blaming the DETECTOR for an APPARATUS error.  Right colour,
regress/gate_offline.sh:1031:#                            wrong diagnosis, which is the F5 shape in selfmutant.py's own
regress/gate_offline.sh:1032:#                            header and the dangerous one of the three.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1033:#    * RELATIVE path      -> SELFMUTANT_OK.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1034:selfmutant_one diff_diskimage_geom.c  src/disk/diskimage.c   diskimage_geom "must not fold to 1 in the SPT position" "-std=gnu99 ../src/disk/diskimage.c"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1035:#  selfmutant6, the last of the five.  ONE CHARACTER: `step <= cnt` -> `step < cnt` in
regress/gate_offline.sh:1036:#  sh4_timer_tick().  Kills exactly 1 of 16 rows, and it is plausible in good faith --
regress/gate_offline.sh:1037:#  dev_sh4.c:208-216 says TCNT counts TCOR..0 and underflows AFTER 0, so reading "reaching
regress/gate_offline.sh:1038:#  zero IS the underflow" gives `<` directly.  That comment exists to refute exactly this,
regress/gate_offline.sh:1039:#  the same shape as diskimage_sync's "fsync already flushes".
regress/gate_offline.sh:1040:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1041:#  The obvious alternative was BUILT AND RUN rather than reasoned about: `period = tcor[i]
regress/gate_offline.sh:1042:#  + 1` -> `tcor[i]` DIES BY SIGNAL 8, because the TCOR=0 steady state reaches the `%
regress/gate_offline.sh:1043:#  period`.  Guard F5 scores that SETUP -- a crash is a FAULT, never a detection.  And
regress/gate_offline.sh:1044:#  `<=` -> `>=` reddens all 16 rows, the "deleting arithmetic breaks its tests" shape the
regress/gate_offline.sh:1045:#  .why files warn against.  No extra CC flags: built under both the gate's line and
regress/gate_offline.sh:1046:#  selfmutant.py's and the outputs are byte-identical.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1047:selfmutant_one diff_sh4_tmu.c        src/devices/dev_sh4.c       sh4_tmu    "boundary"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1048:selfmutant_one diff_rtc_range.c      src/devices/dev_rtc.c       rtc_range  "2^32: timer NOT removed"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1051:#  THE MANIFEST -- the part that stops this being a five-instance fix.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1052:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1053:#  Without it, differential #12 arrives with no control and nobody notices, which
regress/gate_offline.sh:1054:#  is EXACTLY how R4-R9 added five differentials without once touching gate 3.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1055:#  Same idiom as run.sh's GATE_MANIFEST: adding a differential means adding a
regress/gate_offline.sh:1056:#  control or naming it on the exemption list, deliberately.  That is the point.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1057:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1058:#  The exemptions are DATED and NAMED rather than silent.  They are real debt:
regress/gate_offline.sh:1059:#  each is a differential whose rows could all be inert with this gate green.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1060:#  Filed as `selfmutant6` -- doing them silently in this round would break the
regress/gate_offline.sh:1061:#  stopping rule, and leaving them unnamed after writing the helper would repeat
regress/gate_offline.sh:1062:#  the "grep for its siblings" miss this project keeps making.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1063:SM_COVERED="timer memory_rw footbridge m8820x m8invread diskimage_sync diskimage_parse wdc_identify diskimage_io diskimage_geom sh4_tmu rtc_range"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1064:#  DEADLINES SET BY THE OWNER, 2026-08-17, TIGHTER THAN THE ONES I PROPOSED.  I had picked
regress/gate_offline.sh:1065:#  Oct/Nov unilaterally; asked, the owner chose a fortnight -- 148 uncovered rows across five
regress/gate_offline.sh:1066:#  differentials is urgent, not a Q4 item.  Recorded because a deadline nobody chose is a
regress/gate_offline.sh:1067:#  deadline nobody owns, and this gate goes RED on that date whether or not the work is done.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1068:#  fbpending_bound, dated 2026-09-20.  *** THE HARNESS HAS A FAILABILITY CONTROL -- its
regress/gate_offline.sh:1069:#  @@SELFCHECK@@ block is present and the SC manifest covers it -- but the SELF-MUTANT LANE
regress/gate_offline.sh:1070:#  DOES NOT RUN YET, and the two catch different failures, so this is a real gap and not a
regress/gate_offline.sh:1071:#  formality. ***  The lane builds in a MIRRORED tree and this harness is the only one that
regress/gate_offline.sh:1072:#  links against ~221 build objects rather than compiling standalone; its pristine arm builds
regress/gate_offline.sh:1073:#  in the mirror but does not emit the PASS token, and finding out why is its own piece of
regress/gate_offline.sh:1074:#  work rather than a flag.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1075:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1076:#  WHAT IS NOT MISSING, so the exemption is not read as "untested": the mutants were built and
regress/gate_offline.sh:1077:#  run by hand against the tracked tree on 2026-08-20, and every one is killed by a named row.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1078:#    pre-fix          FAIL R1 x7 and R2 with trough -2147483648 (the overflow itself)
regress/gate_offline.sh:1079:#    reset to 0       FAIL R3 x7   (R1 stays green -- the bound still works, only the debt)
regress/gate_offline.sh:1080:#    one callback     FAIL R1 x7
regress/gate_offline.sh:1081:#    no ceiling       FAIL R1 on the two high-emulated_hz cases; R2 peak 2147483647
regress/gate_offline.sh:1082:#  The `.old`/`.new`/`.why` fragments are committed and describe exactly that, so the lane is
regress/gate_offline.sh:1083:#  a wiring job, not a design one.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1084:SM_EXEMPT="ieee_store:2099-01-01 fbpending_bound:2026-09-20"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1093:    for _e in $SM_EXEMPT; do [ "${_e%%:*}" = "$stem" ] && _found=yes; done
regress/gate_offline.sh:1098:#  *** F3: THE MANIFEST VERIFIED THE LEDGER, NOT THE WORK. ***  SM_COVERED is a hand-written
regress/gate_offline.sh:1099:#  string and nothing cross-checked it against the selfmutant_one calls, so a pass-2 seat
regress/gate_offline.sh:1100:#  measured BOTH directions green: delete a selfmutant_one call and leave SM_COVERED intact ->
regress/gate_offline.sh:1101:#  GREEN; move a stem from EXEMPT to COVERED, retune the count, write no mutant -> GREEN, with
regress/gate_offline.sh:1102:#  the gate then PRINTING the paper shrink as evidence.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1103:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1104:#  Same G-vs-H distinction this project already wrote into precommit_check.sh: the manifest was
regress/gate_offline.sh:1105:#  a G with no H.  It verified that a name appeared in a list, never that work stood behind it.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1108:    grep -qE "^selfmutant_one +[^ ]+ +[^ ]+ +$_stem +" "$HERE/gate_offline.sh" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:1111:check "selfmutant manifest: every COVERED stem has a real selfmutant_one call" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:1113:#  ieee_store carries a far-future date because GATE 3 covers it -- against TOTAL comparator
regress/gate_offline.sh:1114:#  death only, and by the vacuity-prone `grep -c PASS == 0` form this helper was written to
regress/gate_offline.sh:1115:#  avoid, so it is a weaker exemption than it looks.  The other five are uncovered debt with
regress/gate_offline.sh:1116:#  real deadlines.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1117:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1118:#  DATED ENTRIES, NOT A COUNT.  The first version asserted `wc -w == 6` under a row named
regress/gate_offline.sh:1119:#  "6, shrinking not growing" -- and a seat measured that SHRINKING IT IS RED (got=5 want=6),
regress/gate_offline.sh:1120:#  so the pin actively obstructed the thing its own name claimed to want, and the name embedded
regress/gate_offline.sh:1121:#  the constant so a legitimate shrink would print "the exemption list is 6 ...   5".  A count
regress/gate_offline.sh:1122:#  pins; a date expires.  Shrinking is now green by construction: there is nothing to retune.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1123:#  *** A MALFORMED EXEMPTION DATE NEVER EXPIRED, AND IT STILL SATISFIED THE COVERAGE CHECK.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1124:#  ***  REPRODUCED against the shipped loop, with both controls green (a well-formed past date
regress/gate_offline.sh:1125:#  DID expire; a well-formed future one did not):
regress/gate_offline.sh:1126:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1127:#      ieee_store            no date at all   -> never expires
regress/gate_offline.sh:1128:#      ieee_store:2026-8-1   unpadded, 19 days past -> never expires
regress/gate_offline.sh:1129:#      ieee_store:garbage    not a date       -> never expires
regress/gate_offline.sh:1130:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1131:#  MECHANISM.  `${e##*:}` strips to the LAST colon -- and with NO colon it strips nothing and
regress/gate_offline.sh:1132:#  returns the whole entry, so `ieee_store` was compared as a date: 's' > '2', false, unexpired.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1133:#  The `""` arm only catches `stem:` with an empty tail, which is the one malformed case that
regress/gate_offline.sh:1134:#  was handled.  For the unpadded form the compare is worse than useless: `2026-8-1` against
regress/gate_offline.sh:1135:#  `2026-08-20` matches through `2026-`, then '8' > '0', so a date 19 days PAST sorts as
regress/gate_offline.sh:1136:#  FUTURE.  And the coverage check keys on `${e%%:*}` -- the stem -- so the exemption is
regress/gate_offline.sh:1137:#  honoured throughout.  The teeth of the exempt arm depended on a format NOTHING VALIDATED.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1138:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1139:#  THE FIX IS TO REFUSE, NOT TO GUESS.  A malformed entry is now RED and names itself; it is
regress/gate_offline.sh:1140:#  never silently treated as unexpired.  A `case` glob rather than a regex, because this runs
regress/gate_offline.sh:1141:#  under POSIX sh and `[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]` needs no external process.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1142:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1143:#  ONE HELPER FOR BOTH MANIFESTS.  SM_EXEMPT and SC_EXEMPT carried byte-identical loops a few
regress/gate_offline.sh:1144:#  hundred lines apart, so a fix to one would have left the other -- which is this project's
regress/gate_offline.sh:1145:#  "grep for the siblings before calling it fixed" rule, and the reason both are converted
regress/gate_offline.sh:1146:#  here in one edit.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1147:exempt_expired () {          # exempt_expired <today> <entries...>
regress/gate_offline.sh:1165:sm_expired=$(exempt_expired "$(date +%Y-%m-%d)" $SM_EXEMPT)
regress/gate_offline.sh:1169:TMUBIN=$LOGDIR/diff_sh4_tmu
regress/gate_offline.sh:1173:        -o "$TMUBIN" "$HERE/diff_sh4_tmu.c" > "$TMULOG" 2>&1; then
regress/gate_offline.sh:1178:    "$TMUBIN" > "$TMULOG" 2>&1
regress/gate_offline.sh:1204:    check     "SH-4 TMU: the three-timer row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:1205:              "$(grep -c 'three timers at once' "$TMULOG")" "1"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1207:    #  table, because gcov showed timer_interrupts_pending[i]++ was NEVER
regress/gate_offline.sh:1217:    check     "SH-4 TMU: the stopped-timer row is present" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:1218:              "$(grep -c 'stopped timer' "$TMULOG")" "1"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1223:# ---- #429: the RTC frequency range check ----------------------------------------------
regress/gate_offline.sh:1224:# *** THIS ROW SAT HELD FOR DAYS ON A RECORDED OBSTACLE THAT WAS MEASURABLY FALSE. ***  The
regress/gate_offline.sh:1225:# filing said a differential "cannot simply #include the device (it pulls in cpu.h,
regress/gate_offline.sh:1226:# machine.h, emul.h, device.h)".  FOUR differentials in this very file already do exactly
regress/gate_offline.sh:1227:# that, and dev_sh4.c -- included by one of them a few hundred lines above -- pulls a strict
regress/gate_offline.sh:1228:# superset of dev_rtc.c's headers.  The obstacle cost more than the round did.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1229:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1230:# WHAT IT DEFENDS.  #429 shipped with NO detector: nothing under regress/ compiled or even
regress/gate_offline.sh:1231:# mentioned dev_rtc.c, so reverting the range check whole, or flipping its comparison, was
regress/gate_offline.sh:1232:# green here.  That is the fifth vacuity class -- a shipped fix whose mutant passes
regress/gate_offline.sh:1233:# everything.  Measured 2026-08-19: the true pre-#429 revert now fails 4 rows and the
regress/gate_offline.sh:1234:# flipped comparison fails 5, both on VALUES rather than by crashing.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1235:# ------------------------------------------------------------------ #442 fbpending
regress/gate_offline.sh:1236:#  THE FOOTBRIDGE PENDING-INTERRUPT COUNTER, bounded.  Unbounded, it overflowed INT_MAX and
regress/gate_offline.sh:1237:#  the emulated clock delivered NOTHING for 2^31 further ticks -- reproduced at rung 3 on an
regress/gate_offline.sh:1238:#  unmodified -E cats, three runs, wrap at ~43 s and a 40 s dead window.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1239:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1240:#  THIS ROW IS A DETECTOR, NOT THE REPRODUCTION, and the distinction is load-bearing here.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1241:#  It #includes the shipped dev_footbridge.c and calls timer_tickN() directly, which the
regress/gate_offline.sh:1242:#  witness ladder's mechanical discriminator classes as never-a-reproduction.  The
regress/gate_offline.sh:1243:#  reproduction is regress/fbpending_drain_probe.py (rung 3) and it is NOT run here.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1244:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1245:#  BOTH SHIP BECAUSE NEITHER ALONE IS ENOUGH -- measured, not assumed:
regress/gate_offline.sh:1246:#    * the rung-3 probe cannot reach the boundary.  The core delivers at most
regress/gate_offline.sh:1247:#      TIMER_MAX_CATCHUP * TIMER_BASE_FREQUENCY = 68,157,440 increments per wall second, so
regress/gate_offline.sh:1248:#      2^31 of them take 31.5 s AT BEST and a gate row cannot spend that.  This harness does
regress/gate_offline.sh:1249:#      2^31 direct increments in ~4 s.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1250:#    * a mutant deleting the absolute ceiling (leaving the bound at the timer's own rate)
regress/gate_offline.sh:1251:#      PASSES the rung-3 probe on every timer and is caught only here, at emulated_hz above
regress/gate_offline.sh:1252:#      68 MHz -- reachable through the documented -I flag.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1253:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1254:#  It links every object EXCEPT main.o and dev_footbridge.o, so it runs the REAL timer.c and
regress/gate_offline.sh:1255:#  the REAL footbridge rather than re-implementations.  Measured kills: pre-fix fails R1 x7
regress/gate_offline.sh:1256:#  and R2 with trough -2147483648 (the overflow itself); reset-to-0 fails R3 x7; a fix applied
regress/gate_offline.sh:1257:#  to one callback only fails R1 x7; no-ceiling fails R1 on the two high-hz cases and R2 with
regress/gate_offline.sh:1258:#  peak 2147483647.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1259:FBBIN=$LOGDIR/diff_fbpending_bound
regress/gate_offline.sh:1260:FBLOG=$LOGDIR/diff_fbpending_bound.log
regress/gate_offline.sh:1263:    note "fbpending bound: no build/ objects -- SKIPPED, not passed"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1265:    if ! $CC -O2 -std=gnu99 -I"$SEC/src/include" -I"$SEC/src/include/thirdparty"             -I"$SEC/src/devices" -o "$FBBIN" "$HERE/diff_fbpending_bound.c"             $FBOBJS -lm -L/usr/X11R6/lib -lX11 > "$FBLOG" 2>&1; then
regress/gate_offline.sh:1266:        note "fbpending bound compile failed:"; sed 's/^/       /' "$FBLOG" | head -12
regress/gate_offline.sh:1267:        check "fbpending bound: links against the real timer.c" "no" "yes"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1269:        check "fbpending bound: links against the real timer.c" "yes" "yes"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1273:        "$FBBIN" 2147487744 > "$FBLOG" 2>&1
regress/gate_offline.sh:1275:        check "fbpending bound: offline verdict" "$(grep -c 'FBBOUND_VERDICT=PASS' "$FBLOG")" "1"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1276:        check "fbpending bound: R2 actually ran (not SKIPPED)"               "$(grep -c 'R2 no signed overflow.*direct ticks' "$FBLOG")" "1"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1277:        check_min "fbpending bound: rows actually run"               "$(grep -cE '^  (ok|FAIL) ' "$FBLOG")" 15
regress/gate_offline.sh:1281:RTCBIN=$LOGDIR/diff_rtc_range
regress/gate_offline.sh:1285:        -o "$RTCBIN" "$HERE/diff_rtc_range.c" > "$RTCLOG" 2>&1; then
regress/gate_offline.sh:1290:    "$RTCBIN" > "$RTCLOG" 2>&1
regress/gate_offline.sh:1297:    #    12 -> 17 the threshold was pinned only from ABOVE, and the timer core's own input
regress/gate_offline.sh:1308:    #  timer core's floor turns into roughly one tick per three years -- the guest asked
regress/gate_offline.sh:1310:    #  in the other direction: zero is the documented way to stop the timer, so a fix that
regress/gate_offline.sh:1313:              "$(grep -c -F '2^32: timer NOT removed' "$RTCLOG")" "1"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1315:              "$(grep -c -F '2^63: timer NOT removed' "$RTCLOG")" "1"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1322:    #  only that it is nonzero, positive, and did not remove the timer.  MEASURED rather than
regress/gate_offline.sh:1331:    #  two lines apart in timer.h -- 12 of 12 green while every guest rate above 2^20 was
regress/gate_offline.sh:1332:    #  boosted to INT_MAX, which timer.h:72-77 itself calls unserviceable.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1338:    #  timer_update_frequency() passed 12 of 12: the detector captured last_timer_hz from the
regress/gate_offline.sh:1340:    check     "RTC range: the timer core's own input is asserted" \
regress/gate_offline.sh:1341:              "$(grep -c -F 'timer core got' "$RTCLOG")" "2"
regress/gate_offline.sh:1344:    #  outside timer.h.  Named because deleting it silently restores the blindness -- and
regress/gate_offline.sh:1351:# ---- #405: the ATA IDENTIFY capacity bytes --------------------------------------
regress/gate_offline.sh:1352:# Third instance of the same construction (float_emul, dev_sh4, now dev_wdc): the driver
regress/gate_offline.sh:1353:# stubs the diskimage_* externals and #includes dev_wdc.c, so the function that runs is
regress/gate_offline.sh:1354:# the one that ships. wdc_initialize_identify_struct() is static, so there is no way to
regress/gate_offline.sh:1355:# link it without including the file.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1356:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1357:# *** WHY THE NAMED ROWS BELOW ARE THE WHOLE POINT. `% 255` and `& 255` return the SAME
regress/gate_offline.sh:1358:# byte for every operand below 255, so a table of plausible-looking disk sizes passes on
regress/gate_offline.sh:1359:# the UNFIXED code -- every disk under 33,423,360 bytes agrees. Each named row puts a
regress/gate_offline.sh:1360:# specific byte at or past the divergence point. Measured against the shipped defect:
regress/gate_offline.sh:1361:# the full revert fails 7 rows, and a PARTIAL fix that corrects only the two `>> 8`
regress/gate_offline.sh:1362:# lines still fails 4 -- which is the case a less careful table would have let through.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1363:# ***
regress/gate_offline.sh:1364:WDCBIN=$LOGDIR/diff_wdc_identify
regress/gate_offline.sh:1368:        -o "$WDCBIN" "$HERE/diff_wdc_identify.c" > "$WDCLOG" 2>&1; then
regress/gate_offline.sh:1373:    "$WDCBIN" > "$WDCLOG" 2>&1
regress/gate_offline.sh:1776:    #                 lived. The stub now poisons BY INVERSION: every id except
regress/gate_offline.sh:1800:# ---- #406: the autodev.c generator ----------------------------------------------
regress/gate_offline.sh:1801:# A prose mention of "DEVINIT" in a comment used to reach the generated C. It cost
regress/gate_offline.sh:1802:# 358 compiler errors, no binary, gate 1 red and eleven gates skipped -- from one
regress/gate_offline.sh:1803:# comment line, which is still in the tree DELIBERATELY (rewording it would have
regress/gate_offline.sh:1804:# made the hardening deletable in silence).
regress/gate_offline.sh:1805:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1806:# The driver runs the REAL generator in a scratch copy, so it cannot drift from
regress/gate_offline.sh:1807:# what ships, and it carries its own mutant lane. The load-bearing row is the
regress/gate_offline.sh:1808:# ORDERED NAME LIST, not a character check: measured, the half-fix that keeps
regress/gate_offline.sh:1809:# noglob but drops the anchor emits 27 extra names of which 26 are DOTLESS --
regress/gate_offline.sh:1810:# valid C identifiers that a "no bad characters" row waves straight through, and
regress/gate_offline.sh:1811:# that fail at link time instead of parse time.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1843:# ---- #412: SCSI READ CAPACITY on a zero-block disk -------------------------------
regress/gate_offline.sh:1844:# `size` is uint64_t and nr_of_logical_blocks is int64_t, so 0 - 1 underflowed and the
regress/gate_offline.sh:1845:# guest was told the disk holds 4,294,967,296 blocks -- 2 TiB -- rather than that it is
regress/gate_offline.sh:1846:# empty. Measured both directions before and after: 0xffffffff -> 0x00000000.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1847:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1848:# THIS IS NOT A CORNER CASE TODAY. A separate live defect leaves every `-d gH;S` disk with
regress/gate_offline.sh:1849:# zero blocks, and such a disk KEEPS its SCSI/IDE type, so it reaches the handler and is told
regress/gate_offline.sh:1850:# it holds 2 TiB. Measured: a 10 MB image announced as "SCSI DISK id 0, 0 MB (CHS=0,16,63)".
regress/gate_offline.sh:1851:# (#413 narrowed this from "every floppy": DISKIMAGE_FLOPPY is a WRITE-ONLY type -- no device
regress/gate_offline.sh:1852:# ever passes it, so a floppy-typed disk never reaches this handler at all. That is a separate
regress/gate_offline.sh:1853:# filed defect. The row below is unaffected; a 0-byte image IS a reachable zero-block disk.) The "0 KB" on the console is the host-side banner only; the guest is told two
regress/gate_offline.sh:1854:# terabytes. This guard keeps an empty disk reporting empty however it got that way, and
regress/gate_offline.sh:1855:# stays correct after that defect is fixed -- which is why its row uses a 0-BYTE IMAGE
regress/gate_offline.sh:1856:# rather than a floppy. A floppy row would go vacuous the moment the geometry fix lands.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1857:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1858:# The driver carries four further sections, DISABLED, that assert defects still live
regress/gate_offline.sh:1859:# (short/failed writes reported as success, reads past EOF, and a single WRITE(10) that
regress/gate_offline.sh:1860:# grew a 10 KB image to 512 MB with status GOOD). Build with -DDISKIMAGE_IO_UNFIXED to
regress/gate_offline.sh:1861:# see them fail; the round that fixes them deletes the guard.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1862:#  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
regress/gate_offline.sh:1863:#  #437: SYNCHRONIZE CACHE durability.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1864:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1865:#  --wrap=fsync,--wrap=fflush is NOT optional decoration.  The bigger half of #437 is that
regress/gate_offline.sh:1866:#  an overlaid disk never fsynced the file holding the guest's data, and NO byte comparison
regress/gate_offline.sh:1867:#  can see that: measured this round, a plain fflush with no fsync leaves the bytes fully
regress/gate_offline.sh:1868:#  visible to a separate reader after _exit(), because the page cache is the kernel's and
regress/gate_offline.sh:1869:#  outlives the process.  Only real power loss distinguishes fsync, so the CALL COUNTS are
regress/gate_offline.sh:1870:#  the evidence.  The flags FAIL CLOSED, which was measured rather than assumed: drop both
regress/gate_offline.sh:1871:#  and the harness still links but reports 7 rows / 4 failed; drop only --wrap=fflush and it
regress/gate_offline.sh:1872:#  reports 2 failed.  An earlier version of this comment claimed it would "still pass against
regress/gate_offline.sh:1873:#  nothing", which is the opposite of the truth and would have taught the next reader to
regress/gate_offline.sh:1874:#  distrust a red row.
regress/gate_offline.sh:1875:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:1876:#  Differential verified BOTH WAYS before this block was written: 7 rows / 0 failed against
regress/gate_offline.sh:1877:#  the fix, 7 rows / 5 failed against the pre-fix sources taken from git.  The pre-fix arm
regress/gate_offline.sh:1878:#  had to be built with the detector INSIDE the pre-fix tree: a quoted relative
regress/gate_offline.sh:1879:#  #include "../src/disk/diskimage.c" resolves against the including file's own directory,
regress/gate_offline.sh:1880:#  so compiling it from $SEC pulled in the FIXED source and reported a meaningless pass.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2036:# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
regress/gate_offline.sh:2037:# #414: disk GEOMETRY differential -- diskimage_recalc_size() and the -d gH;S
regress/gate_offline.sh:2038:# parser, linked against the real diskimage.c.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2039:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:2040:# It needs a WRITABLE DIRECTORY because recalc_size() calls stat(): every row
regress/gate_offline.sh:2041:# creates a real file of an exact size rather than faking the size field, so
regress/gate_offline.sh:2042:# what is measured is the shipped code path and not a stub of it.  GEOMDIR is
regress/gate_offline.sh:2043:# how the driver is told where to put them.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2044:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:2045:# The row count is asserted, not merely reported.  Two files named
regress/gate_offline.sh:2046:# diff_diskimage_geom.c existed during development in paths differing only by
regress/gate_offline.sh:2047:# the case of a parent directory, and the smaller one encoded a design decision
regress/gate_offline.sh:2048:# that had since been reversed; a run that silently used the wrong one looked
regress/gate_offline.sh:2049:# exactly like a run that used the right one.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2091:# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
regress/gate_offline.sh:2092:# #418: the `-d` PREFIX PARSER differential.  Offline, no binary, no guest.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2093:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:2094:# `d:` is documented as the escape hatch out of the size heuristic, but nothing
regress/gate_offline.sh:2095:# acted on it, so a floppy-sized image stayed DISKIMAGE_FLOPPY -- a type NO
regress/gate_offline.sh:2096:# controller ever passes -- and the guest got no disk at all.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2097:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:2098:# WHY THE [SIZE] ROWS BELOW ARE NAMED INDIVIDUALLY.  Measured, six variants
regress/gate_offline.sh:2099:# built and run: a placement in the prefix block ALSO makes `d:` work and is
regress/gate_offline.sh:2100:# killed by NOTHING ELSE IN THE FILE -- only the four exactness rows fail it,
regress/gate_offline.sh:2101:# because that placement runs before diskimage_recalc_size() and rounds the
regress/gate_offline.sh:2102:# advertised capacity up (720K 1440 -> 2016 blocks, +40%).  Delete those four
regress/gate_offline.sh:2103:# rows and the wrong placement ships green.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2144:# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
regress/gate_offline.sh:2145:#  THE SELFCHECK MANIFEST (selfcheck7).  The SECOND failability control, brought up to the
regress/gate_offline.sh:2146:#  standard the self-mutant manifest above already reached.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2147:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:2148:#  The two controls cover DIFFERENT failures and neither substitutes for the other; the long
regress/gate_offline.sh:2149:#  form is at the head of the selfmutant section.  In one line each: the self-mutant breaks
regress/gate_offline.sh:2150:#  the SUBJECT and demands a NAMED row notice, so it catches a fixture that has stopped
regress/gate_offline.sh:2151:#  reaching the code; the @@SELFCHECK@@ sentinel breaks nothing and feeds the detector's OWN
regress/gate_offline.sh:2152:#  comparator a deliberate mismatch, so it catches a comparator edited into always-passing --
regress/gate_offline.sh:2153:#  a state in which every row prints `ok`, and the row count, the identity row and the verdict
regress/gate_offline.sh:2154:#  token ALL SURVIVE, so the self-mutant and every other assertion here stay green.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2155:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:2156:#  WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT BESIDE ITS SIBLING: three of the four assertions read the
regress/gate_offline.sh:2157:#  FILESYSTEM and could live anywhere, but the fourth reads each differential's LOG, and six
regress/gate_offline.sh:2158:#  of the twelve do not run until after the selfmutant block.  $LOGDIR persists between runs,
regress/gate_offline.sh:2159:#  so asserting it there would grade the previous run's logs.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2160:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:2161:#  *** THE THIRD ASSERTION IS THE H, AND IT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE ROUND. ***  A name in
regress/gate_offline.sh:2162:#  SC_COVERED is a LEDGER ENTRY; sc_unbacked greps the differential's OWN SOURCE for a real
regress/gate_offline.sh:2163:#  @@SELFCHECK@@ block, so moving a stem from EXEMPT to COVERED and writing no sentinel is
regress/gate_offline.sh:2164:#  RED.  Without it this would be another G -- a check that a name appears in a list -- which
regress/gate_offline.sh:2165:#  is precisely the defect being corrected here.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2166:SC_COVERED="timer memory_rw footbridge m8820x m8invread diskimage_sync diskimage_parse diskimage_io diskimage_geom sh4_tmu rtc_range fbpending_bound"
regress/gate_offline.sh:2167:#  *** COVERED IS NOT UNIFORM, AND THE MANIFEST CANNOT SEE THE DIFFERENCE -- READ THIS BEFORE
regress/gate_offline.sh:2168:#  TREATING A GREEN ROW AS "the file is vouched for". ***  All but three of the COVERED stems
regress/gate_offline.sh:2169:#  route every row through one or two shared helpers, so the sentinel covers the file.  The
regress/gate_offline.sh:2170:#  count used to be spelled out here as "seven of the ten" and went stale the first time a
regress/gate_offline.sh:2171:#  stem was added; a proportion that has to be re-derived on every addition is a comment that
regress/gate_offline.sh:2172:#  will be wrong more often than right, so it is stated as an exception list instead.  The
regress/gate_offline.sh:2173:#  three that do NOT route through shared helpers:
regress/gate_offline.sh:2174:#    * sh4_tmu   -- 12 of 18 rows.  row1() is the only comparator in the file that takes an
regress/gate_offline.sh:2175:#                   expected value; row_three_timers/row_interrupt/row_interrupt_unie_clear/
regress/gate_offline.sh:2176:#                   row_stopped/row_no_freeze each hardcode their own, and cannot be probed.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2177:#    * geom      -- every row but the identity row, via BOTH geom() and parse(), which are
regress/gate_offline.sh:2178:#                   two separate comparators and are probed separately for that reason.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2179:#    * sync      -- both checks() and checkn(), for the same reason.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2180:#  rtc_range is in the UNIFORM group: every row goes through chk(), its single comparator.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2181:#  Named here rather than left to be re-derived, because the three-file exception list above
regress/gate_offline.sh:2182:#  is only trustworthy if additions are classified as they arrive.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2183:#  The per-file comments carry the scope; this is a LIVENESS control, never a census, and
regress/gate_offline.sh:2184:#  "going from 0 to 1 is not false comfort; calling 1 `the detector works` is".
regress/gate_offline.sh:2185:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:2186:#  THE EXEMPTIONS, DATED AND REASONED.  A FORTNIGHT, matching the deadline the owner chose
regress/gate_offline.sh:2187:#  over the Q4 one I proposed for the selfmutant list: 2 uncovered differentials, 1311 and 766
regress/gate_offline.sh:2188:#  lines, whose every row could be inert with this gate green.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2189:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:2190:#    ieee_store    HAS NO COMPARATOR TO SENTINEL.  No check(), no `rows`, no `failures`: each
regress/gate_offline.sh:2191:#                  section carries its own counter (abs_bad, rm_rn_bad, rm_rz_bad, rm_d_bad,
regress/gate_offline.sh:2192:#                  rm_vec_bad, interp_bad) with the comparison written inline dozens of times,
regress/gate_offline.sh:2193:#                  and this gate greps one summary line per counter for 0.  There is no single
regress/gate_offline.sh:2194:#                  point to feed a mismatch to.  A probe through a comparator NO REAL ROW USES
regress/gate_offline.sh:2195:#                  would prove nothing while reading exactly like coverage, so none was
regress/gate_offline.sh:2196:#                  written -- a forced control is worse than none.  Gate 3 does cover this file
regress/gate_offline.sh:2197:#                  against TOTAL comparator death, which is this control's own failure mode,
regress/gate_offline.sh:2198:#                  but by the vacuity-prone `grep -c PASS == 0` form its SM_EXEMPT comment
regress/gate_offline.sh:2199:#                  already flags, so it is a weaker cover than it looks.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2200:#    wdc_identify  NOTHING TO FEED A MISMATCH TO.  Read every row function: row_anchor,
regress/gate_offline.sh:2201:#                  row_slave, row_base_drive, row_identity, row_atapi_flags, row_wide_geometry,
regress/gate_offline.sh:2202:#                  row_no_lba_claim and row_selfconsistent are ZERO-ARGUMENT with the expected
regress/gate_offline.sh:2203:#                  value hardcoded in the body, and row(why, sectors) derives its expectation
regress/gate_offline.sh:2204:#                  FROM ITS INPUT (`got57 != sectors`), so changing the input changes the
regress/gate_offline.sh:2205:#                  answer too and no mismatch can be produced.  Injecting one means mutating
regress/gate_offline.sh:2206:#                  dev_wdc.c -- which is the OTHER control, already present.  This one needs
regress/gate_offline.sh:2207:#                  the nine row functions refactored onto a shared helper first: a round, not
regress/gate_offline.sh:2208:#                  a line.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2224:#  THE H.  Grep the SOURCE, not the ledger and not the log: a stem can only be COVERED if
regress/gate_offline.sh:2225:#  its own file contains the block.  @@SELFCHECK@@ rather than the summary text, because the
regress/gate_offline.sh:2226:#  token is what selfmutant.py filters and what the probe rows are named with, so a block
regress/gate_offline.sh:2227:#  gutted down to its printf still fails this.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2236:#  DATED ENTRIES, NOT A COUNT -- the selfmutant list learned this the hard way: `wc -w == 6`
regress/gate_offline.sh:2237:#  under a row named "shrinking not growing" made SHRINKING IT RED.  A count pins; a date
regress/gate_offline.sh:2238:#  expires, and shrinking is green by construction.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2239:#  The SM manifest's twin, and it carried the identical defect -- see exempt_expired() above.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2240:sc_expired=$(exempt_expired "$(date +%Y-%m-%d)" $SC_EXEMPT)
regress/gate_offline.sh:2244:#  THE G -- and it is not made redundant by the H above.  The H proves the block EXISTS in the
regress/gate_offline.sh:2245:#  source; this proves it RAN, which is a different failure: a block behind a dead #ifdef, a
regress/gate_offline.sh:2246:#  differential whose compile failed, or a driver that died before reaching it all satisfy the
regress/gate_offline.sh:2247:#  H and fail this.  Assert the OK-token is PRESENT rather than "no FAIL line", because a
regress/gate_offline.sh:2248:#  detector that never started prints neither and a negative form is satisfied by a control
regress/gate_offline.sh:2249:#  that never ran.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2258:# ------------------------------------------------------------------ #446 sgi_eaddr
regress/gate_offline.sh:2259:#  MOVED TO GATE 9 (gate_asan_sweep) AND DELIBERATELY NOT REPLACED HERE.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2260:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:2261:#  It was wired into this gate because the detector was pure SOURCE TEXT and so needed
regress/gate_offline.sh:2262:#  neither a binary nor a rig.  A pass-2 panel then built sixteen mutants against that
regress/gate_offline.sh:2263:#  detector and ALL SIXTEEN SCORED 7/7 -- including a two-character edit that restores
regress/gate_offline.sh:2264:#  the heap-buffer-overflow with no compiler warning, and a 217-byte file containing
regress/gate_offline.sh:2265:#  nothing but a C comment.  The replacement is a RUNTIME value oracle: it breaks on
regress/gate_offline.sh:2266:#  arcbios_init() in a real construction of five real subtypes and requires the ethernet
regress/gate_offline.sh:2267:#  string to BE the formatting of the MAC bytes passed beside it.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2268:#
regress/gate_offline.sh:2269:#  That needs build/gxemul and gdb, and THIS GATE RUNS NO EMULATOR AT ALL -- it is the
regress/gate_offline.sh:2270:#  offline differential of the linked float_emul.c, and it references the binary nowhere.
regress/gate_offline.sh:2271:#  Adding an emulator run here would have made the gate's own name untrue, which is the
