   1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
   2: """`fbpending`: a RUNG-3 cold-debugger witness that the emulated footbridge clock
   3: STOPS for 42.9 s in every 85.9 s, on an UNMODIFIED in-tree `-E cats`.
   4: 
   5: THE DEFECT.  dev_footbridge.c:73 declares `int pending_timer_interrupts[4]`; the four
   6: callbacks at :86-92 do `->pending_timer_interrupts[N] ++` with no bound; and
   7: DEVICE_TICK(footbridge) at :259-277 delivers a tick ONLY under `if (... [i] > 0)`.
   8: Nothing decrements the counter except a guest write to TIMER_x_CLEAR, so a guest that
   9: programs a fast timer and does not acknowledge drives the counter to INT_MAX, where `++`
  10: is signed overflow (UB) and, in practice, wraps to INT_MIN.  For the next 2^31
  11: increments the `> 0` test is FALSE: no INTERRUPT_ASSERT, and timer_value stops being
  12: re-randomised.
  13: 
  14: THE RATE, arithmetic this probe measures rather than assumes.  The increments happen in
  15: the SIGALRM handler (core/timer.c:254-290), at up to TIMER_MAX_CATCHUP (1048576) per
  16: timer per signal, TIMER_BASE_FREQUENCY (65.0 Hz) signals per second -- a ceiling of
  17: 68,157,440 increments per WALL-CLOCK second.  The footbridge timer's own rate is
  18: machine->emulated_hz / effective_cycles, and cats sets emulated_hz = 50,000,000
  19: (machine_cats.c:56-57), so TIMER_1_LOAD = 1 asks for 50e6 Hz, under the ceiling and
  20: therefore delivered in full.  2^31 / 50e6 = 42.95 s to the wrap and 2^32 / 50e6 =
  21: 85.90 s to the return, giving the 42.9-in-85.9 duty cycle this probe plots.
  22: 
  23: THE GUEST-VISIBLE DISCRIMINATOR, and why it needs no interrupt plumbing.  TIMER_1_VALUE
  24: (dev_footbridge.c:557-562) is READ/WRITE: a read returns d->timer_value[0], a write
  25: stores `idata & TIMER_MAX_VAL`.  DEVICE_TICK overwrites that same field with
  26: `random() % footbridge_effective_cycles(load, control, 0)` -- and with load == 1 that
  27: modulus is 1, so EVERY delivered tick writes exactly 0.  So the guest:
  28: 
  29:     writes 0x00AB0000 into TIMER_1_VALUE, then polls it.
  30:     It reads back 0x00AB0000 ... until a tick lands, and then reads 0.
  31: 
  32: Each 0 is one delivered tick, counted by the guest into RAM.  Pre-wrap that count climbs
  33: at the device-tick rate; post-wrap it FREEZES and the sentinel the guest wrote survives
  34: indefinitely.  No interrupt controller, no IRQ enable (which would hit an unrelated
  35: fatal()+exit(1) at dev_footbridge.c:486-499), no guest OS, no disk image.
  36: 
  37: *** WHY A FROZEN COUNTER IS NOT MERELY "THE EMULATOR STOPPED", which is the whole
  38:     difficulty of a negative measurement.  Three quantities run CONCURRENTLY in the same
  39:     guest loop and are published to separate RAM words every iteration: ***
  40: 
  41:       0x9024  iteration count   -- climbs whether or not the device does anything.  If
  42:                                    this freezes, the EMULATOR stopped and the whole
  43:                                    measurement is void.  It is a liveness control.
  44:       0x902c  VENDOR_ID re-read -- a fresh guest ldr from footbridge+0x00 on EVERY
  45:                                    iteration.  0x1011 here during the frozen window
  46:                                    proves the device is still MAPPED and still
  47:                                    DISPATCHING; what stopped is the TICK, not the
  48:                                    device.  RAM cannot produce 0x1011.
  49:       0x9020  delivered ticks   -- the thing under test.
  50: 
  51:     A freeze in 0x9020 while 0x9024 climbs and 0x902c still reads 0x1011 is a statement
  52:     about dev_footbridge.c and about nothing else.
  53: 
  54: THE MMU TRAP (documented by footbridge_sites_probe.py, whose arm T this reuses).
  55: machine_cats.c calls arm_setup_initial_translation_table(), which maps only the low
  56: 256 MB and IGNORES the top VA nibble.  The footbridge is at PA 0x42000000, outside that
  57: window, so under the boot MMU every device read returns 0 with a RAM liveness row still
  58: green.  Hence arm T: the same program with the three MMU-disable words replaced by nops
  59: MUST NOT produce the device's answers.
  60: 
  61: ARMS
  62:   A  the witness.  MMU off, TIMER_1_LOAD=1, TIMER_1_CONTROL=TIMER_ENABLE, then
  63:      free-running in wall-clock slices with the counters sampled out of RAM between
  64:      them.  Default 23 x 5 s = 115 s of free-run, which brackets BOTH the wrap at
  65:      42.95 s and the return at 85.90 s.
  66:   E  encodings.  Byte-identical to A except ONE immediate: the sentinel is 0 instead of
  67:      0x00AB0000, which makes the poll compare equal on the first pass and therefore
  68:      walks EVERY instruction of both loop bodies under `step 1`.  That is what lets the
  69:      hand-assembled words of the loop -- the words actually under test -- be checked
  70:      against the disassembly the emulator prints as it EXECUTES them, rather than
  71:      against a separate `unassemble` of words that might never run.
  72:   T  negative control.  MMU left on.  Must not return 0x1011 or 0x00345678.
  73:   K  failability control.  Arm A's prologue plus ONE instruction, a guest read of
  74:      IRQ_ENABLE_SET (footbridge+0x188), which dev_footbridge.c:486-499 answers with
  75:      fatal() then exit(1).  A probe in which nothing can die proves nothing by
  76:      surviving; K must kill the host and A must not.
  77: 
  78: CONTROLS ARE NOT OPTIONAL AND ABSENT DATA MUST FAIL.  Every RAM word is poisoned with
  79: 0xDEADBEEF before the run, every verdict row first requires the values it consults to be
  80: PRESENT, and an arm that did not reach SURVIVED voids every row that reads it.  This
  81: project has shipped a row that reported ok by comparing two -1 defaults.
  82: 
  83: PRE-FIX / POST-FIX POLARITY.  There is no fix at the time of writing, so what this file
  84: can show is the FIRST clause of witness validity only: the symptom on the committed
  85: build.  It is written so the SAME file is the post-fix detector -- FBPENDING_VERDICT=PASS
  86: means the controls held AND the run was LONG ENOUGH to see the window AND the clock never
  87: stopped.
  88: 
  89: *** A RUN THAT WAS TOO SHORT MUST NOT CERTIFY A FIX, which is "absent data must fail"
  90:     applied to the TIME axis and is a trap this probe walked into while being written.
  91:     At 11 x 5 s the wrap is reached at 43.1 s and only 10 s of dead window fits inside
  92:     the run, which is under the freeze threshold -- so the probe printed SYMPTOM=ABSENT,
  93:     VERDICT=PASS, exit 0, on a build with the defect fully present.  A shorter run than
  94:     that would not have reached the wrap at all and would have looked even cleaner.
  95:     Hence FBPENDING_COVERAGE: absence is only reportable once the free-run total has
  96:     passed the predicted RETURN at 85.90 s, and otherwise the symptom is UNDETERMINED
  97:     rather than ABSENT. ***
  98: 
  99: KNOWN BLIND SPOT, stated rather than discovered later.  This measures the guest-visible
 100: SYMPTOM, not the undefined behaviour.  A "fix" that merely made the counter unsigned
 101: would remove the freeze and pass every row here while leaving a wrap in place; only a
 102: sanitizer build (regress/build_asan.sh does pass -fsanitize=address,undefined) or a row
 103: that inspects the counter's own bound can speak to that.
 104: 
