 949: #  and the difference matters.  memory_rw's flag makes BOTH arms consistently wrong, so the
 950: #  control cannot see it.  Here, omitting the flags makes the pristine arm fail to PASS, so
 951: #  selfmutant.py reports SELFMUTANT_SETUP -- which its own header says is NEVER scored as a
 952: #  detection.  Measured both ways.  The earlier comment asserted the memory_rw shape and
 953: #  taught the wrong hazard class.
 954: selfmutant_one diff_diskimage_sync.c src/disk/diskimage.c   diskimage_sync "overlay: sync flushes+syncs data" "-Wl,--wrap=fsync -Wl,--wrap=fflush"
 955: #  selfmutant6, part 1 of 3.  Both mutants were chosen so that exactly ONE row sees them --
 956: #  measured, 1 of 37 and 1 of 16 -- which is the diskimage_sync criterion: a mutant that
 957: #  reddens five rows proves little beyond "deleting code breaks tests".
 958: #
 959: #  diskimage_parse drops `&& !prefix_s` from #418's escape-hatch guard.  THIS IS THE SEVENTH
 960: #  MUTANT OF #418 PASS 2: a reading seat proposed it, it was executed, and it SURVIVED all 35
 961: #  rows -- the row it now dies on was added to kill it.  On every rig this project boots the
 962: #  term genuinely is redundant, because get_default_disk_type_for_machine() returns SCSI for
 963: #  PMAX/ARC/SGI/LUNA88K/MVME88K, so `sd:` resolves to SCSI either way.  It bites only on an
 964: #  IDE-default machine -- which is exactly what makes "this term is redundant" a plausible
 965: #  good-faith edit rather than sabotage.
 966: #
 967: #  -std=gnu99 is the memory_rw hazard class, found by reading the compile lines rather than
 968: #  by being bitten: gate_offline.sh builds this harness with -std=gnu99 while selfmutant.py's
 969: #  hardcoded CC uses -std=c99, and EXTRA_FLAGS is appended so the trailing one wins.  MEASURED
 970: #  green BOTH ways, so it is belt-and-braces -- but it makes the control compile what the
 971: #  battery compiles, which is the entire reason that flag argument exists.
 972: selfmutant_one diff_diskimage_parse.c src/disk/diskimage.c    diskimage_parse "'sd:' stays SCSI" "-std=gnu99"
 973: #  wdc_identify zeroes word 6's high byte on the premise that sectors-per-track is an 8-bit
 974: #  field.  THIS IS THE MUTANT #410 MEASURED SURVIVING AT ALL FIVE OPTIMISATION LEVELS: #409
 975: #  shipped row_wide_geometry with s=17, so word 6's high byte was permanently zero while the
 976: #  row's name claimed words 3 AND 6.  The maintainer's premise is false for the reason
 977: #  dev_wdc.c's own bucket (b) gives -- `-d f:` yields spt 256 and 1280.  Deliberately NOT the
 978: #  `% 255` revert, which reddens seven rows.
 979: selfmutant_one diff_wdc_identify.c    src/devices/dev_wdc.c   wdc_identify    "geometry words carry their high byte"
 980: #  selfmutant6, part 2.  THE I/O AND GEOMETRY DIFFERENTIALS, 79 rows between them.
 981: #
 982: #  *** BOTH NEEDED A VERDICT TOKEN BEFORE THEY COULD BE CONTROLLED AT ALL, and that is a
 983: #  FINDING rather than a chore. ***  selfmutant.py asserts the FAIL token is PRESENT --
 984: #  never that PASS is absent -- so a differential printing only a row table can be broken
 985: #  in every row without the helper being able to say so.  diff_diskimage_geom.c printed
 986: #  DISKIMAGE_GEOM_PASS and NOTHING on failure; diff_diskimage_io.c printed no token in
 987: #  either direction.  MEASURED with the tokens missing: geom's mutant arm reported
 988: #  SELFMUTANT_FAIL, "the detector did NOT report a failure", while its named row was in
 989: #  fact RED -- the helper blaming the detector for an apparatus gap -- and io could not
 990: #  get past its pristine arm at all (SELFMUTANT_SETUP).
 991: #
 992: #  I/O.  The mutant drops the READ-SIDE ZERO-FILL from #416's bound and KEEPS the bound,
 993: #  so the 43 rows that assert "the access was refused" all stay GREEN and only the two
 994: #  that assert what happens to the CALLER'S BYTES go red (MEASURED: 45 rows / 0 failures
 995: #  pristine, 45 / 2 mutant).  Every mutant of the bound ITSELF -- advertised capacity
 996: #  instead of the backed extent, a start-only bound, dropping `offset < 0` -- reddens four
 997: #  to six rows across three sections and proves only that deleting a bound breaks the rows
 998: #  written for it.  diskimage.c:1469-1473 exists to refute this mutant's premise ("we
 999: #  return failure, so the caller must not look at the buffer"): dev_wdc's read path
1000: #  discards this function's return value and copies a 32 KB STACK buffer to the guest.
1001: #  -std=gnu99 is the memory_rw hazard class again -- MEASURED green both ways, supplied so
1002: #  the lane compiles what the battery compiles rather than something adjacent to it.
1003: #
1004: #  The one-row alternative that was measured and NOT taken is recorded in the .why file:
1005: #  `lendone < (ssize_t)len` -> `lendone <= 0` kills exactly one row, but its kill depends
1006: #  on RLIMIT_FSIZE producing a genuine PARTIAL write, so on a host that absorbs 0 bytes
1007: #  instead of 256 the mutant survives and the control goes red for an environmental
1008: #  reason that reads exactly like a dead detector.  gate_ab's wall-clock oracle in
1009: #  miniature; determinism was preferred to a one-row pin.
1010: selfmutant_one diff_diskimage_io.c    src/disk/diskimage.c   diskimage_io   "refused read leaves NO caller bytes" "-std=gnu99"
1011: #  GEOMETRY.  strtoll -> strtoull at the SPT parse site ONLY; the heads field five lines
1012: #  above keeps strtoll, which is what makes it a plausible half-done edit rather than a
1013: #  rewrite.  strtoull applies a leading minus in UNSIGNED arithmetic, so
1014: #  `-d g16;-18446744073709551615:` folds to spt=1 and is ACCEPTED.  The premise -- "heads
1015: #  and sectors per track are counts, they cannot be negative, use the unsigned parse" --
1016: #  is the good-faith kind.  diff_diskimage_geom.c's own FAMILY C note (:605) records that
1017: #  this passed all 30 rows of that file's previous version.  MEASURED here: 34 rows / 0
1018: #  failures pristine, 34 / 1 mutant -- one red row, and it is the pinned one.
1019: #
1020: #  *** THE EXTRA ARGUMENT IS A SOURCE FILE, NOT A FLAG, AND IT IS A THIRD HAZARD CLASS
1021: #  THAT NEITHER OF THE TWO ALREADY RECORDED IN THIS FILE COVERS. ***  Alone among these
1022: #  differentials, geom does NOT #include its subject: the battery compiles it against
1023: #  "$SEC/src/disk/diskimage.c" as a second translation unit, so the helper must be told to
1024: #  do the same, and the path must be RELATIVE because it is resolved from the mutant
1025: #  tree's own regress/ directory.  MEASURED all three ways:
1026: #    * omitted            -> link error -> SELFMUTANT_SETUP.  The diskimage_sync class:
1027: #                            the pristine arm cannot PASS, and SETUP is never a detection.
1028: #    * ABSOLUTE $SEC path -> the PRISTINE subject is compiled into BOTH arms, the mutant
1029: #                            survives, and the helper prints SELFMUTANT_FAIL -- red, but
1030: #                            blaming the DETECTOR for an APPARATUS error.  Right colour,
1031: #                            wrong diagnosis, which is the F5 shape in selfmutant.py's own
1032: #                            header and the dangerous one of the three.
1033: #    * RELATIVE path      -> SELFMUTANT_OK.
1034: selfmutant_one diff_diskimage_geom.c  src/disk/diskimage.c   diskimage_geom "must not fold to 1 in the SPT position" "-std=gnu99 ../src/disk/diskimage.c"
1035: #  selfmutant6, the last of the five.  ONE CHARACTER: `step <= cnt` -> `step < cnt` in
1036: #  sh4_timer_tick().  Kills exactly 1 of 16 rows, and it is plausible in good faith --
1037: #  dev_sh4.c:208-216 says TCNT counts TCOR..0 and underflows AFTER 0, so reading "reaching
1038: #  zero IS the underflow" gives `<` directly.  That comment exists to refute exactly this,
1039: #  the same shape as diskimage_sync's "fsync already flushes".
1040: #
1041: #  The obvious alternative was BUILT AND RUN rather than reasoned about: `period = tcor[i]
1042: #  + 1` -> `tcor[i]` DIES BY SIGNAL 8, because the TCOR=0 steady state reaches the `%
1043: #  period`.  Guard F5 scores that SETUP -- a crash is a FAULT, never a detection.  And
1044: #  `<=` -> `>=` reddens all 16 rows, the "deleting arithmetic breaks its tests" shape the
1045: #  .why files warn against.  No extra CC flags: built under both the gate's line and
1046: #  selfmutant.py's and the outputs are byte-identical.
1047: selfmutant_one diff_sh4_tmu.c        src/devices/dev_sh4.c       sh4_tmu    "boundary"
1048: selfmutant_one diff_rtc_range.c      src/devices/dev_rtc.c       rtc_range  "2^32: timer NOT removed"
1049: ' ' ') -lm -L/usr/X11R6/lib -lX11"
1050: 
1051: #  THE MANIFEST -- the part that stops this being a five-instance fix.
1052: #
1053: #  Without it, differential #12 arrives with no control and nobody notices, which
1054: #  is EXACTLY how R4-R9 added five differentials without once touching gate 3.
1055: #  Same idiom as run.sh's GATE_MANIFEST: adding a differential means adding a
1056: #  control or naming it on the exemption list, deliberately.  That is the point.
1057: #
1058: #  The exemptions are DATED and NAMED rather than silent.  They are real debt:
1059: #  each is a differential whose rows could all be inert with this gate green.
1060: #  Filed as `selfmutant6` -- doing them silently in this round would break the
1061: #  stopping rule, and leaving them unnamed after writing the helper would repeat
1062: #  the "grep for its siblings" miss this project keeps making.
1063: SM_COVERED="timer memory_rw footbridge m8820x m8invread diskimage_sync diskimage_parse wdc_identify diskimage_io diskimage_geom sh4_tmu rtc_range"
1064: #  DEADLINES SET BY THE OWNER, 2026-08-17, TIGHTER THAN THE ONES I PROPOSED.  I had picked
1065: #  Oct/Nov unilaterally; asked, the owner chose a fortnight -- 148 uncovered rows across five
1066: #  differentials is urgent, not a Q4 item.  Recorded because a deadline nobody chose is a
1067: #  deadline nobody owns, and this gate goes RED on that date whether or not the work is done.
1068: #  fbpending_bound, dated 2026-09-20.  *** THE HARNESS HAS A FAILABILITY CONTROL -- its
1069: #  @@SELFCHECK@@ block is present and the SC manifest covers it -- but the SELF-MUTANT LANE
1070: #  DOES NOT RUN YET, and the two catch different failures, so this is a real gap and not a
1071: #  formality. ***  The lane builds in a MIRRORED tree and this harness is the only one that
1072: #  links against ~221 build objects rather than compiling standalone; its pristine arm builds
1073: #  in the mirror but does not emit the PASS token, and finding out why is its own piece of
1074: #  work rather than a flag.
1075: #
1076: #  WHAT IS NOT MISSING, so the exemption is not read as "untested": the mutants were built and
1077: #  run by hand against the tracked tree on 2026-08-20, and every one is killed by a named row.
1078: #    pre-fix          FAIL R1 x7 and R2 with trough -2147483648 (the overflow itself)
1079: #    reset to 0       FAIL R3 x7   (R1 stays green -- the bound still works, only the debt)
1080: #    one callback     FAIL R1 x7
1081: #    no ceiling       FAIL R1 on the two high-emulated_hz cases; R2 peak 2147483647
1082: #  The `.old`/`.new`/`.why` fragments are committed and describe exactly that, so the lane is
1083: #  a wiring job, not a design one.
1084: SM_EXEMPT="ieee_store:2099-01-01 fbpending_bound:2026-09-20"
1085: sm_missing=""
1086: for f in "$HERE"/diff_*.c; do
1087:     stem=$(basename "$f" .c); stem=${stem#diff_}
1088:     #  Match the STEM, not the whole token: exemptions now carry ":date" suffixes, and a
1089:     #  substring match against the raw string would silently accept a stem that is merely a
1090:     #  prefix of another one.
1091:     _found=no
1092:     for _c in $SM_COVERED; do [ "$_c" = "$stem" ] && _found=yes; done
1093:     for _e in $SM_EXEMPT; do [ "${_e%%:*}" = "$stem" ] && _found=yes; done
1094:     [ "$_found" = yes ] || sm_missing="$sm_missing $stem"
1095: done
1096: check "selfmutant manifest: every differential is covered or dated-exempt" \
1097:       "${sm_missing:-none}" "none"
1098: #  *** F3: THE MANIFEST VERIFIED THE LEDGER, NOT THE WORK. ***  SM_COVERED is a hand-written
1099: #  string and nothing cross-checked it against the selfmutant_one calls, so a pass-2 seat
1100: #  measured BOTH directions green: delete a selfmutant_one call and leave SM_COVERED intact ->
1101: #  GREEN; move a stem from EXEMPT to COVERED, retune the count, write no mutant -> GREEN, with
1102: #  the gate then PRINTING the paper shrink as evidence.
1103: #
1104: #  Same G-vs-H distinction this project already wrote into precommit_check.sh: the manifest was
1105: #  a G with no H.  It verified that a name appeared in a list, never that work stood behind it.
1106: sm_unbacked=""
1107: for _stem in $SM_COVERED; do
1108:     grep -qE "^selfmutant_one +[^ ]+ +[^ ]+ +$_stem +" "$HERE/gate_offline.sh" \
1109:         || sm_unbacked="$sm_unbacked $_stem"
1110: done
1111: check "selfmutant manifest: every COVERED stem has a real selfmutant_one call" \
1112:       "${sm_unbacked:-none}" "none"
1113: #  ieee_store carries a far-future date because GATE 3 covers it -- against TOTAL comparator
1114: #  death only, and by the vacuity-prone `grep -c PASS == 0` form this helper was written to
1115: #  avoid, so it is a weaker exemption than it looks.  The other five are uncovered debt with
1116: #  real deadlines.
1117: #
1118: #  DATED ENTRIES, NOT A COUNT.  The first version asserted `wc -w == 6` under a row named
1119: #  "6, shrinking not growing" -- and a seat measured that SHRINKING IT IS RED (got=5 want=6),
1120: #  so the pin actively obstructed the thing its own name claimed to want, and the name embedded
1121: #  the constant so a legitimate shrink would print "the exemption list is 6 ...   5".  A count
1122: #  pins; a date expires.  Shrinking is now green by construction: there is nothing to retune.
1123: #  *** A MALFORMED EXEMPTION DATE NEVER EXPIRED, AND IT STILL SATISFIED THE COVERAGE CHECK.
1124: #  ***  REPRODUCED against the shipped loop, with both controls green (a well-formed past date
1125: #  DID expire; a well-formed future one did not):
1126: #
1127: #      ieee_store            no date at all   -> never expires
1128: #      ieee_store:2026-8-1   unpadded, 19 days past -> never expires
1129: #      ieee_store:garbage    not a date       -> never expires
1130: #
1131: #  MECHANISM.  `${e##*:}` strips to the LAST colon -- and with NO colon it strips nothing and
1132: #  returns the whole entry, so `ieee_store` was compared as a date: 's' > '2', false, unexpired.
1133: #  The `""` arm only catches `stem:` with an empty tail, which is the one malformed case that
1134: #  was handled.  For the unpadded form the compare is worse than useless: `2026-8-1` against
1135: #  `2026-08-20` matches through `2026-`, then '8' > '0', so a date 19 days PAST sorts as
1136: #  FUTURE.  And the coverage check keys on `${e%%:*}` -- the stem -- so the exemption is
1137: #  honoured throughout.  The teeth of the exempt arm depended on a format NOTHING VALIDATED.
1138: #
1139: #  THE FIX IS TO REFUSE, NOT TO GUESS.  A malformed entry is now RED and names itself; it is
1140: #  never silently treated as unexpired.  A `case` glob rather than a regex, because this runs
1141: #  under POSIX sh and `[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]` needs no external process.
1142: #
1143: #  ONE HELPER FOR BOTH MANIFESTS.  SM_EXEMPT and SC_EXEMPT carried byte-identical loops a few
1144: #  hundred lines apart, so a fix to one would have left the other -- which is this project's
1145: #  "grep for the siblings before calling it fixed" rule, and the reason both are converted
1146: #  here in one edit.
1147: exempt_expired () {          # exempt_expired <today> <entries...>
1148:     local today="$1"; shift
1149:     local out="" e d
1150:     for e in "$@"; do
1151:         case "$e" in
1152:             *:*) ;;
1153:             *) out="$out $e(NO-DATE)"; continue ;;
1154:         esac
1155:         d=${e##*:}
1156:         case "$d" in
1157:             [0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]) ;;
1158:             *) out="$out ${e%%:*}(BAD-DATE:${d:-empty})"; continue ;;
1159:         esac
1160:         [ "$d" \< "$today" ] && out="$out ${e%%:*}"
1161:     done
1162:     printf '%s' "${out:-none}"
1163: }
1164: 
1165: sm_expired=$(exempt_expired "$(date +%Y-%m-%d)" $SM_EXEMPT)
1166: check "selfmutant manifest: no exemption is past its dated deadline OR malformed" \
1167:       "$sm_expired" "none"
1168: 
1169: TMUBIN=$LOGDIR/diff_sh4_tmu
1170: TMULOG=$LOGDIR/diff_sh4_tmu.log
1171: if ! $CC -O2 -std=c99 -I"$SEC/src/include" -I"$SEC/src/include/thirdparty" \
1172:         -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -Wl,--gc-sections \
1173:         -o "$TMUBIN" "$HERE/diff_sh4_tmu.c" > "$TMULOG" 2>&1; then
1174:     note "SH-4 TMU differential compile failed:"; sed 's/^/       /' "$TMULOG" | head -12
1175:     check "SH-4 TMU: compiles against the real dev_sh4.c" "no" "yes"
1176: else
1177:     check "SH-4 TMU: compiles against the real dev_sh4.c" "yes" "yes"
1178:     "$TMUBIN" > "$TMULOG" 2>&1
1179:     sed 's/^/       /' "$TMULOG"
1180:     #  Assert the verdict AND the row count. The verdict alone would stay green if the
1181:     #  table were emptied; a floor on the rows is what stops that.
1182:     check     "SH-4 TMU: row failures" \
1183:               "$(grep -oE '[0-9]+ failures' "$TMULOG" | grep -oE '^[0-9]+')" "0"
1184:     #  Floor raised 16 -> 18: 17 for the UNIE-clear row, 18 once the identity row was
1185:     #  added and counted itself.  (This comment said "16 -> 17" for half an hour while
1186:     #  the value below read 18 -- a number matching neither, caught by the ninth review
1187:     #  seat.  A comment that names a constant goes stale the moment the constant moves.)
1188:     #  A minimum the current value already
1189:     #  exceeds is decoration rather than evidence -- the reason #416 raised the diskimage
1190:     #  I/O floor from 3 to 45.
1191:     check_min "SH-4 TMU: rows actually run" \
1192:               "$(grep -oE '^[0-9]+ rows' "$TMULOG" | grep -oE '^[0-9]+')" 18
1193:     check     "SH-4 TMU: offline verdict" "$(grep -c 'SH4_TMU_PASS' "$TMULOG")" "1"
1194:     #  #401: the multi-period rows are named because they are the ONLY rows that
1195:     #  make the modulo mean anything. Four seats independently found that dropping
1196:     #  `% period` passed all of #400's eleven rows -- none ever reached
1197:     #  remaining >= period, so the correction itself went untested. Deleting these
1198:     #  must be visible rather than silent.
1199:     check     "SH-4 TMU: the multi-period rows are present" \
1200:               "$(grep -c 'multi-period wrap' "$TMULOG")" "2"
1201:     #  Named rows, so that deleting one is visible rather than silent.
1202:     check     "SH-4 TMU: the freeze row is present" \
1203:               "$(grep -c 'no freeze past 515.4 s' "$TMULOG")" "1"
1204:     check     "SH-4 TMU: the three-timer row is present" \
1205:               "$(grep -c 'three timers at once' "$TMULOG")" "1"
1206:     #  #403: a seat found FOURTEEN wrong implementations that passed the earlier
1207:     #  table, because gcov showed timer_interrupts_pending[i]++ was NEVER
1208:     #  EXECUTED -- no row set TCR_UNIE. These three rows are what made deleting
1209:     #  the increment, wiping TCR with `=`, ignoring TSTR, and a signed compare
1210:     #  visible. Name them so removing one is loud.
1211:     check     "SH-4 TMU: the interrupt row is present" \
1212:               "$(grep -c 'underflow raises one interrupt' "$TMULOG")" "1"
1213:     #  The OTHER HALF of this harness's own claim.  MEASURED: without this row, deleting
1214:     #  the `if (d->tcr[i] & TCR_UNIE)` guard at dev_sh4.c:249 left all sixteen rows GREEN.
1215:     check     "SH-4 TMU: the UNIE-clear row is present" \
1216:               "$(grep -c 'UNIE clear raises no interrupt' "$TMULOG")" "1"
1217:     check     "SH-4 TMU: the stopped-timer row is present" \
1218:               "$(grep -c 'stopped timer' "$TMULOG")" "1"
1219:     check     "SH-4 TMU: the reset-default row is present" \
1220:               "$(grep -c 'reset default must not underflow' "$TMULOG")" "1"
1221: fi
1222: 
1223: # ---- #429: the RTC frequency range check ----------------------------------------------
1224: # *** THIS ROW SAT HELD FOR DAYS ON A RECORDED OBSTACLE THAT WAS MEASURABLY FALSE. ***  The
1225: # filing said a differential "cannot simply #include the device (it pulls in cpu.h,
1226: # machine.h, emul.h, device.h)".  FOUR differentials in this very file already do exactly
1227: # that, and dev_sh4.c -- included by one of them a few hundred lines above -- pulls a strict
1228: # superset of dev_rtc.c's headers.  The obstacle cost more than the round did.
1229: #
1230: # WHAT IT DEFENDS.  #429 shipped with NO detector: nothing under regress/ compiled or even
1231: # mentioned dev_rtc.c, so reverting the range check whole, or flipping its comparison, was
1232: # green here.  That is the fifth vacuity class -- a shipped fix whose mutant passes
1233: # everything.  Measured 2026-08-19: the true pre-#429 revert now fails 4 rows and the
1234: # flipped comparison fails 5, both on VALUES rather than by crashing.
1235: # ------------------------------------------------------------------ #442 fbpending
1236: #  THE FOOTBRIDGE PENDING-INTERRUPT COUNTER, bounded.  Unbounded, it overflowed INT_MAX and
1237: #  the emulated clock delivered NOTHING for 2^31 further ticks -- reproduced at rung 3 on an
1238: #  unmodified -E cats, three runs, wrap at ~43 s and a 40 s dead window.
1239: #
1240: #  THIS ROW IS A DETECTOR, NOT THE REPRODUCTION, and the distinction is load-bearing here.
1241: #  It #includes the shipped dev_footbridge.c and calls timer_tickN() directly, which the
1242: #  witness ladder's mechanical discriminator classes as never-a-reproduction.  The
1243: #  reproduction is regress/fbpending_drain_probe.py (rung 3) and it is NOT run here.
1244: #
1245: #  BOTH SHIP BECAUSE NEITHER ALONE IS ENOUGH -- measured, not assumed:
1246: #    * the rung-3 probe cannot reach the boundary.  The core delivers at most
1247: #      TIMER_MAX_CATCHUP * TIMER_BASE_FREQUENCY = 68,157,440 increments per wall second, so
1248: #      2^31 of them take 31.5 s AT BEST and a gate row cannot spend that.  This harness does
1249: #      2^31 direct increments in ~4 s.
1250: #    * a mutant deleting the absolute ceiling (leaving the bound at the timer's own rate)
1251: #      PASSES the rung-3 probe on every timer and is caught only here, at emulated_hz above
1252: #      68 MHz -- reachable through the documented -I flag.
1253: #
1254: #  It links every object EXCEPT main.o and dev_footbridge.o, so it runs the REAL timer.c and
1255: #  the REAL footbridge rather than re-implementations.  Measured kills: pre-fix fails R1 x7
1256: #  and R2 with trough -2147483648 (the overflow itself); reset-to-0 fails R3 x7; a fix applied
1257: #  to one callback only fails R1 x7; no-ceiling fails R1 on the two high-hz cases and R2 with
1258: #  peak 2147483647.
1259: FBBIN=$LOGDIR/diff_fbpending_bound
1260: FBLOG=$LOGDIR/diff_fbpending_bound.log
1261: FBOBJS=$(find "$SEC/../build/src" -name '*.o' ! -name 'main.o' ! -name 'dev_footbridge.o' 2>/dev/null | sort)
1262: if [ -z "$FBOBJS" ]; then
1263:     note "fbpending bound: no build/ objects -- SKIPPED, not passed"
1264: else
1265:     if ! $CC -O2 -std=gnu99 -I"$SEC/src/include" -I"$SEC/src/include/thirdparty"             -I"$SEC/src/devices" -o "$FBBIN" "$HERE/diff_fbpending_bound.c"             $FBOBJS -lm -L/usr/X11R6/lib -lX11 > "$FBLOG" 2>&1; then
1266:         note "fbpending bound compile failed:"; sed 's/^/       /' "$FBLOG" | head -12
