 509: #
 510: #  Committing it is the mrwstore2 lesson: that round's rung-3 probe left NO ARTEFACT, so its
 511: #  witness became a remembered grep and a later row had to re-cost the work.  #442's witness had
 512: #  the same problem and was committed one commit ago for the same reason.
 513: EXPECT_CONVERTED=23
 514: #  One helper for all three, so they agree on WHAT they look at.  The first draft
 515: #  used py_code() for the anchored count and a raw grep for the other two, which
 516: #  meant a comment mentioning the echo guard would have inflated one count and not
 517: #  the others -- a spurious mismatch with no defect behind it.  A seat caught it
 518: #  while it was still latent.
 519: #  Same scope as py_code above -- the two must agree, and a past defect came from
 520: #  their populations diverging.
 521: probe_code() { find "$HERE" -name '*.py' -type f ! -path "$HERE/artifacts/*" \
 522:                     ! -name 'readiness_predicate_test.py' -print0 2>/dev/null | \
 523:                xargs -0 grep -h "$1" 2>/dev/null | grep -v '^[[:space:]]*#'; }
 524: conv_anchor=$(probe_code 'resp.rstrip().endswith("GXemul>")' | grep -c .)
 525: conv_echo=$(probe_code 'echo is not None and echo not in resp' | grep -c .)
 526: #  ANCHOR THROUGH THE ECHO ARGUMENT.  The first version stopped at
 527: #  'return wait(mark=_mark' -- a PREFIX -- so deleting ", echo=s if s else None"
 528: #  from all fourteen sites left this count at 14 and every other counter
 529: #  unchanged. A pass-2 seat MEASURED that mutant passing gate_hygiene,
 530: #  gate_offline, gate_mips_rounding and gate_sh_rounding simultaneously: a
 531: #  shipped fix with no detector, which is this project's worst vacuity class.
 532: #  The comma is what makes the assertion an assertion.
 533: #  ANCHOR THROUGH THE WHOLE EXPRESSION, not just as far as "echo=". A pass-2b
 534: #  seat pointed out that the previous form still admitted
 535: #      return wait(mark=_mark, echo=None)
 536: #  which passes the argument and disables the guard in ONE WORD. This is the
 537: #  THIRD tightening of this same count -- first it stopped at "_mark", then at
 538: #  "echo=", now at the full conditional. Each time the surviving prefix was a
 539: #  real substring of both the fixed and the broken code, which is exactly the
 540: #  property a detector must not have.
 541: #  *** EXTENDED 2026-08-20 FOR THE METHOD FORM, and narrowly on purpose given the history
 542: #  in the paragraph above. ***  Every probe until now wrote `send()` as a closure over a local
 543: #  `buf`, so the guard read `return wait(mark=_mark, ...)`.  fbpending_drain_probe.py is the
 544: #  first CLASS-BASED probe in the tree: its guard is `return self.wait(mark=_mark, ..., 
 545: #  timeout=timeout)`, which is not a spelling variation but a different construct, and the
 546: #  pattern could not see it AT ALL -- the guard was present, correct, and uncounted.
 547: #
 548: #  That is a wider blind spot than the `mark`/`_mark` one filed as sh4markspell: a whole probe
 549: #  STYLE was invisible.  The two additions here are `\(self\.\)\?` and an optional trailing
 550: #  argument.  Both are anchored -- `mark=_mark` and the full `echo=` conditional still have to
 551: #  be present and in order -- so neither re-opens the hole the three earlier tightenings closed:
 552: #  a site that passes the argument, or drops the conditional, still fails to match.
 553: conv_mark=$(probe_code 'return \(self\.\)\?wait(mark=_mark, echo=[a-z][a-z]* if [a-z][a-z]* else None[,)]' | grep -c .)
 554: 
 555: #  THE HOLE A PASS-2 SEAT FOUND, and it is the sharpest finding of the review:
 556: #  the checks above catch a REVERT but not an ADDITION of the OTHER broken form.
 557: #  A brand-new site spelled
 558: #        if buf.rstrip().endswith("GXemul>"):
 559: #  passes everything -- bare is still 0, the spelling is RECOGNISED so `unknown`
