wiringcliff -- THE EXEMPTION SET AND THE EXIT-PATH QUESTION, DERIVED 2026-09-09
Derived by running the commands below against HEAD b4d4177, not transcribed from any record.
Three of this project's own records disagreed about this row; all three are corrected here.

================================================================================
1. HOW MANY EXEMPTIONS, AND WHEN DO THEY RED
================================================================================
DERIVER:  grep -n '2026-09-20' tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py

  SIX, all dated 2026-09-20:
      fbpending_drain_probe.py      hpcmips_ctor_probe.py
      wdc_altoffset_probe.py        wdc_altoffset_sig_probe.py
      wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe.py   wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py

  and the checker says so itself in its own comment: "STALE EXEMPTION.  Six exemptions remain."

*** THE LEDGER TITLE SAYS **EIGHT** AND IS WRONG. ***  It reads "EIGHT probe-wiring exemptions
expire on 2026-09-20 together and the checker hard-fails -- four are cheap, only four need a
design round" -- a count that also fails to add up against itself (4 + 4 = 8 against a
population of 6).  The count class this project already tracks: a transcribed number in a
title, never re-derived.

*** AND THE DATE IS OFF BY ONE, IN THE SAFE DIRECTION. ***  The comparison is `d < today', so an
exemption dated 2026-09-20 is still VALID ON 2026-09-20 and first reds on **2026-09-21**.
`_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md' said "a SCHEDULED GATE FAILURE from 2026-09-20".  Corrected.

================================================================================
2. WHICH OF THE SIX CAN ACTUALLY FAIL  -- the load-bearing question
================================================================================
DERIVER:  grep -nE 'sys\.exit\(' <the six>   +  read the last four lines of each

  probe                        how it ends                      status on FAILURE
  ---------------------------  -------------------------------  ------------------
  fbpending_drain_probe.py     sys.exit(0 if ok else 1)  (x2)    ENFORCEABLE
  hpcmips_ctor_probe.py        sys.exit(main());                 ENFORCEABLE
                               main() -> `return 1 if fails else 0'
  wdc_altoffset_probe.py       bare `main()', no sys.exit        *** ALWAYS 0 ***
  wdc_altoffset_sig_probe.py   writes TABLE_C.txt, falls off end *** ALWAYS 0 ***
  wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe.py  writes TABLE_4BIT.txt, falls off  *** ALWAYS 0 ***
  wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py      bare `main()', no sys.exit        *** ALWAYS 0 ***

  **FOUR OF SIX HAVE NO NONZERO EXIT PATH AT ALL.**  Each prints its own completion sentinel
  (ALTOFF_SIG_DONE, ALTOFF_4BIT_DONE, ADDTOINBUF_RUNG3_DONE) and returns 0 whatever it measured.

  *** SO WIRING THEM AS-IS SHIPS FOUR GATE SECTIONS THAT CANNOT FAIL. ***  That is this
  project's own check-that-cannot-fail class -- "a cheap substitute for a real test, which
  returns the same answer whether or not the thing it checks is true" -- and the deadline would
  install four of them in one commit.  **THE JOB IS NOT WIRING.**

*** CHECKPOINT.md SAID "three of the six" AND THAT IS WRONG TOO; IT IS FOUR. ***  The codex seat
(regress/artifacts/wiringcliff/codex_analysis_20260908.err, verdict block present, no refusal)
had it right: "two probes with enforceable verdicts and four WDC measurement harnesses.  All
four WDC harnesses can finish with exit status zero after reporting failures."  That answer was
sitting UNREAD -- the row carried ZERO ledger entries -- which is this project's third-most
expensive recorded class, and it was right where the record was wrong.

================================================================================
3. A DERIVER OF MINE MEASURED A CORRELATE, AND IT IS RECORDED BECAUSE IT NEARLY LANDED
================================================================================
My first pass counted `grep -cE 'exit\([1-9]'` and reported nonzero-exit paths of 1, 0, 1, 1 for
the four WDC harnesses -- i.e. it said three of them COULD fail.  Opening the matches:

    wdc_altoffset_probe.py:415    os._exit(127)     <- the pty CHILD after a failed execv
    wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py:631   os._exit(127)     <- same
    wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe.py:23  "...the old exit(1) used to gate..."   <- A COMMENT

Neither `os._exit(127)` in a forked child gives the PARENT a failure status, and the third is a
QUOTATION of a removed call.  **The class is defined by "does the parent process exit nonzero
when a row fails", and the regex tested the text `exit(` instead** -- the correlate-not-the-
property failure, in the deriver I wrote while correcting someone else's count.  The honest
deriver is `sys\.exit\(` plus reading how the file ENDS; both are in section 2.
(It is also the quotation-vs-assertion collision: a comment quoting the call it removed.)

================================================================================
4. WHAT THIS LEAVES OPEN  -- not answered here
================================================================================
* For each of the four, WHAT should it assert?  Each prints measurements; none states a
  pass/fail contract.  That is a design question per probe, and it is the real content of this
  row -- the reason the ledger's own "four need a design round" half is right even though its
  count is wrong.
* codex adds two facts worth carrying: `gate_wdc.sh' DOES NOT EXIST, and it counts SIXTEEN
  gates in run.sh.  Existing homes it names: gate_arm.sh (CATS stub + Footbridge),
  gate_asan_sweep.sh (machine construction), gate_sh_rounding.sh (the WDC section).
  NEITHER re-derived here; both are codex readings, flagged as such.
* Setup costs differ and that bears on scheduling: the signature probe needs CD-ROM attachment
  semantics but no special disk contents, while the add-to-input-buffer probe requires prepared
  media with known contents.  codex reading, not measured here.

================================================================================
5. *** THE "FOUR ARE CHEAP" HALF IS ALSO WRONG: **ZERO** OF THE SIX IS A CHEAP WIRING. ***
   Derived 2026-09-09 by reading the EXEMPT entries' own reason strings.
================================================================================
The ledger title says "four are cheap, only four need a design round", and the 2026-09-09
adjudication inherited it as "wiringcliff's four CHEAP exemptions must land before the deadline,
as a small side-commit regardless of ranking".  **There is no such side-commit available.**
Every one of the six carries a reason naming a PREREQUISITE THAT DOES NOT EXIST TODAY:

  fbpending_drain_probe.py     "REPORT-ONLY by default: EXPECT_CAP must be set before a gate row
                                can mean anything, SO WIRING IS A DESIGN STEP FIRST"
  hpcmips_ctor_probe.py        "rung 2, ~8 s, no rig image -- WANTS A CONSTRUCTION GATE"
  wdc_altoffset_probe.py       "two-leg differential; WANTS A SINGLE-LEG MODE before gate 10 can
                                assert anything"
  wdc_altoffset_sig_probe.py   "two-leg differential AND NEEDS A ciR: CD-ROM RIG the battery does
                                not build today"
  wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe.py  "two-leg differential; shares the single-leg-mode debt"
  wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py      "two-leg differential AND NEEDS A POISONED CD IMAGE the battery
                                does not build"

*** AND THE REASONS ARE STILL TRUE. ***  Nothing in the tree has changed to discharge any of them:
no gate builds a second leg, no single-leg mode exists, the battery builds neither the CD-ROM rig
nor a poisoned image, and fbpending's EXPECT_CAP still defaults to -1 (report-only).

**THE USEFUL SPLIT IS NOT cheap/expensive.  IT IS CONTROLLABLE vs INFRASTRUCTURE-BLOCKED:**

  CONTROLLABLE (2)  fbpending, hpcmips -- blocked on decisions THIS PROJECT MAKES (what cap is
                    justified; which gate hosts a construction probe).  Both already have a real
                    nonzero exit path, so once the decision is made the wiring IS a line.
  INFRA-BLOCKED (4) the four WDC harnesses -- blocked on an instrument mode and on rig images the
                    battery does not build, AND none has a nonzero exit path (section 2).  Each
                    needs BOTH a single-leg mode AND a pass/fail contract before wiring means
                    anything.

So the deadline arithmetic is: SIX design steps in the remaining days, two of them needing rig
images that do not exist -- not "four cheap wirings plus four design rounds".

*** THE DISPOSITION IS AN OWNER DECISION AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN HERE. ***  The honest
options are (a) re-date the exemptions, carrying the evidence that each blocker still stands, or
(b) six design rounds.  (a) is what a dated-debt table is FOR -- but re-dating is also exactly the
failure a dated-debt table exists to prevent, and this checker's own header states the standard it
must be held to: **"an EXEMPTION naming the round that owes it.  Debt that expires, not debt that
disappears."**  A re-date that does not name the round owing each item converts (a) into (b)'s
opposite -- debt that disappears -- so if (a) is chosen, each entry gains the owing round's id.

*Recorded rather than decided.  What IS decided by measurement is that the "small side-commit" in
the ranking cannot be executed as described, so the calendar override needs re-planning, not
scheduling.*

================================================================================
6. *** MY OWN SECTION-2 CLAIM IS TOO BROAD, AND THE codex SEAT CORRECTED IT.
   codex_contracts_20260909.txt/.err, fired 2026-09-09 with the premise stated AS A QUESTION. ***
================================================================================
Section 2 says the four WDC harnesses have "NO nonzero exit path".  codex checked it and refined
it, which is what stating a premise as a question is FOR:

  "Premise 2 is correct about the absence of `sys.exit(', but 'cannot exit nonzero' is too broad.
   The main altoffset file has two uncaught parent-process assertions: label width and distinct
   result registers ... Both companion altoffset files execute those assertions through their
   import.  Missing arguments or malformed leg specifications can also raise exceptions in the
   companions; uncaught filesystem and PTY errors can terminate all four."

  "Neither PTY implementation propagates the child's exit status.  Failed row comparisons and
   unsuccessful breakpoint observations have no explicit nonzero exit path. ... Thus your
   conclusion holds for an exit-status-only gate evaluating completed measurements."

**THE CORRECT STATEMENT, and the distinction is load-bearing:** these files CAN exit nonzero -- on
an uncaught exception (a bad argument, a missing file, a PTY error, a violated table-integrity
assert).  What they cannot do is exit nonzero because a MEASUREMENT FAILED.  So the gate-section
consequence is unchanged -- a wired section still cannot fail on the thing it exists to test --
but the sentence "no nonzero exit path" is false as written and would be reused.  *An APPARATUS
failure and a MEASUREMENT failure must not render the same, which is this project's oldest rule
about blanks arriving at exit statuses.*

================================================================================
7. THE ACTIONABLE FINDING: ONE OF THE FOUR ALREADY COMPUTES ITS OWN VERDICT
================================================================================
codex, on wdc_altoffset_probe.py: **"`ok` already combines zero row failures, both breakpoint
observations, and complete instruction-word agreement.  It excludes mnemonic agreement and the
entry-PC observation.  Its printed verdict is consequently a real computed decision, although it
never controls exit status."**  All 18 rows carry explicit expectations (RAM 0x55, cylinder
0x55/0x66, FIFO 0x5a/0xa5, ST_POWERON 0x51, ST_ARMED 0x58, ST_IDLE 0x50/0xff, and the defect rows
0x50/0xff baseline vs 0x58/0x71 fixed).

  RANKING (codex): CLOSEST to a pass/fail contract is `wdc_altoffset_probe.py'; FURTHEST is
  `wdc_altoffset_sig_probe.py'.

So the single-leg-mode debt is the real blocker for this one, NOT the absence of an oracle -- the
oracle exists and is computed.  That materially changes section 5's "six design steps": for
wdc_altoffset_probe.py the design step is the LEG question alone.

