VERDICT: NOT READY TO DISCHARGE. The packet conflates two probes, and adding a selfmutant call alone cannot work: the helper does not recognize this differential’s verdict tokens.

S1 is only partly correct. [fbpending_witness.py](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/fbpending_witness.py:121) defaults to **23 × 5 = 115 seconds** for arm A, plus a five-second arm T. Coverage requires approximately **90.9 seconds**, not 86. Its twelve control rows are R0, C1, C2, A1, A2, E, K, T, D1, L1, L2 and L3. It has no report-only mode; `--learn` adds disassembly output without changing the verdict contract. `--expect-cap` and the eleven-row report-only contract belong to **fbpending_drain_probe.py**.

The witness’s actual exit contract agrees with the packet: controls OK, coverage OK and symptom ABSENT produce PASS and exit zero.

For Q1, [selfmutant_one](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/gate_offline.sh:891) takes:

```text
harness subject stem row-id [extra-cc-flags]
```

Two existing calls, quoted exactly:

```sh
selfmutant_one diff_memory_rw.c  src/cpus/memory_rw.c        memory_rw  "G1 " "-fno-optimize-sibling-calls"
selfmutant_one diff_footbridge.c src/devices/dev_footbridge.c footbridge "A1 "
```

The stem selects `.old` and `.new`; the function does not consume `.why`. The fragment contents, using escaped tabs and newline to show their exact bytes, are:

```text
.old: "\t\td->pending_timer_interrupts[nr] = 1;\n"
.new: "\t\td->pending_timer_interrupts[nr] = 0;\n"
```

I verified that the old fragment matches the current subject exactly once.

[fbpending_bound.why](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/selfmutants/fbpending_bound.why:3) chooses reset-to-zero because it preserves the bound while discarding the remaining interrupt debt. It explicitly prefers this over deleting the bound. It specifies **`R4 every case bounded`** as the pin because R3 appears seven times and the helper requires exactly one matching row. It contains **no compiler-flag warning**.

One sentence in `.why` is itself misleading: its claim that a “verdict-only” assertion would accept this mutant contradicts the implementation. Failing R3 increments `failures` and makes the overall verdict FAIL.

The build question needs three distinctions:

- The ordinary differential command at line 1265 supplies the device include directory, build objects and X11 linkage. I counted **221 qualifying objects**, excluding `main.o` and `dev_footbridge.o`. **I did not verify a successful link.**
- A bare four-argument selfmutant call lacks that compilation setup.
- Even with correct linking, [selfmutant.py](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/selfmutant.py:273) recognizes `_PASS` and `_FAIL`, whereas the differential emits `FBBOUND_VERDICT=PASS/FAIL`. I exercised those exact regexes: neither existing verdict matches.

These are the proposed edits necessary for the lane.

Immediately after the existing verdict `printf` in [diff_fbpending_bound.c](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/diff_fbpending_bound.c:361), add:

```c
	printf("FBBOUND_%s\n", failures ? "FAIL" : "PASS");
```

This preserves the gate’s existing token and supplies the helper’s token. It adds no counted `row()`.

Delete this literal stray line at `gate_offline.sh:1049`:

```text
' ' ') -lm -L/usr/X11R6/lib -lX11"
```

After the existing `rtc_range` selfmutant call, insert:

```sh
FBSMOBJS=$(find "$SEC/../build/src" -name '*.o' ! -name 'main.o' ! -name 'dev_footbridge.o' 2>/dev/null | sort | tr '\n' ' ')
if [ -z "$FBSMOBJS" ]; then
    degrade "fbpending selfmutant: build objects missing -- lane did not run"
