The selfmutant helper runs its binary **without arguments**, so its R2 remains skipped; R3 and the aggregate R4 exercise this mutant. The ordinary gate retains its deep invocation with `2147487744`.

S2’s quoted “wiring job” comment and manifest checks exist. They do not establish that the lane works. No expiry-count adjustment is needed: `exempt_expired` checks entries individually, with strict `<` comparison; a September 20 exemption first expires September 21.

For Q2, **none of the three candidates literally satisfies S3**:

| Gate | What the current file actually does |
|---|---|
| `gate_hygiene.sh` | Scans source and existing boot logs. It runs no long guest and has no emulator binary or stub variable. Its fbpending comment concerns readiness bookkeeping. |
| `gate_offline.sh` | Runs offline differentials, not emulator guests; it also has early exit paths. |
| `gate_ab.sh` | Runs long guests, but retains wall-clock stall/backstop decisions and a HEAD-versus-prebatch backstop assertion. |

My proposed existing-gate host is **gate_ab.sh, serialized after all existing A/B work**. That prevents the new workload from perturbing earlier measurements. It is an explicit correction to the literal “gate has no wall-clock oracle” premise.

There is another necessary edit: [gate_ab.sh:66](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/gate_ab.sh:66) calls `need_exec`, which exits through `gate_skip`. An appended witness would otherwise disappear whenever a baseline binary is missing.

